const html: string = "<h2>Poster Abstracts <span class=\"sp-count\">85</span></h2><p class=\"sp-note\">Submissions are grouped by presentation type. Each entry includes its abstract title, author names, institution, and abstract body.</p><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-1\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 1</p><h3>RENAL TRANSPLANTATION WITH DUPLEX URETER: OUR EXPERIENCE</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Varun H U, Dr Bhagwati chandra verma, Dr deepak kumar Rathi, Dr Anuj kunar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MEDANTA GURGAON</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p># RENAL TRANSPLANTATION WITH DUPLEX URETER: OUR EXPERIENCE</p><p>**Objective:**<br/>\nDuplex ureters in donor kidneys are uncommon and may pose technical challenges during renal transplantation. Various techniques have been described, including pantaloon ureteric anastomosis with common-channel implantation and separate ureteroneocystostomy. We present our experience with separate implantation of duplex ureters in five renal transplant recipients.</p><p>**Materials and Methods:**<br/>\nFive renal transplant recipients receiving grafts with complete duplex ureters were included. In all cases, the two donor ureters were implanted separately into the urinary bladder through two different cystotomies, with individual DJ stenting. Patients were followed for graft function and urological outcomes.</p><p>**Results:**<br/>\nAll five recipients underwent successful renal transplantation. Four patients demonstrated good graft function with an uneventful postoperative course and satisfactory follow-up. One patient developed graft dysfunction secondary to renal allograft rejection, with no evidence of a urological abnormality. The adverse graft outcome was therefore considered immunological rather than related to the ureteric reconstruction. Compared with the pantaloon technique, separate ureteroneocystostomy avoids an additional ureter-to-ureter anastomosis and permits independent drainage of both ureters, although it requires two separate bladder implantation sites.</p><p>**Conclusion:**<br/>\nRenal transplantation with complete duplex ureters is feasible with favourable outcomes. Separate ureteroneocystostomy is a simple and effective approach that avoids additional ureteric anastomosis and provides independent drainage of both ureters. In our five-case experience, outcomes were favourable, with the only adverse graft outcome attributable to immunological rejection rather than a urological complication.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-2\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 2</p><h3>Laparoscopic Onlay Graft Ureteroplasty for Long-Segment UPJ Stricture in a Solitary Functioning Kidney: Surgical Management and Graft Selection Analysis</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shikhar Agarwal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Hims</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Long-segment ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) strictures adjacent to major retroperitoneal vasculature present formidable reconstructive challenges. In solitary functioning kidneys, conventional dismembered pyeloplasty carries substantial risks of extensive devascularization, excessive anastomotic tension, and catastrophic vascular injury. Autologous onlay graft ureteroplasty provides an optimal organ-preserving alternative to restore luminal caliber without circumferential mobilization; however, the biological properties and clinical pitfalls of graft selection critically dictate long-term surgical success.<br/>\nMaterial and Methods: A 58-year-old female with a solitary functioning right kidney (contralateral non-functioning kidney secondary to chronic calculous obstruction) presented with progressive right flank pain. Non-contrast computed tomography and intraoperative retrograde pyelography confirmed severe Grade 4 hydronephrosis with a long-segment right UPJ stricture densely adherent to the inferior vena cava. Under transperitoneal laparoscopy, meticulous ureterolysis was performed. The strictured UPJ and proximal ureter were longitudinally incised over a 6-French double-J stent. A tailored full-thickness autologous onlay graft was harvested and sutured to the spatulated defect using continuous 4-0 absorbable sutures, followed by vascularized tissue wrapping.<br/>\nResults: Laparoscopic onlay ureteroplasty achieved complete anatomical reconstitution and unobstructed urinary drainage with zero intraoperative complications, safely preserving total renal function. Literature synthesis and translational comparison revealed profound graft performance distinctions: buccal mucosal grafts (BMG) exhibit non-keratinized stratified epithelium, rapid neovascularization via a dense subepithelial capillary plexus, minimal shrinkage (&lt;10%), zero lithogenicity, and superior patency rates (&gt;90%). Conversely, full-thickness skin grafts (FTSG) suffer progressive dermal contraction (30%–50%), keratin maceration, epithelial desquamation, and luminal hair-follicle stone formation.<br/>\nConclusion: Laparoscopic onlay graft ureteroplasty is a highly feasible, safe, and effective nephron-sparing strategy for vessel-adherent, long-segment UPJ strictures in high-risk solitary kidneys. Buccal mucosa represents the gold-standard graft material over cutaneous grafts to ensure long-term durability.<br/>\nKeywords: Laparoscopic Ureteroplasty, UPJ Stricture, Onlay Graft, Buccal Mucosa, Solitary Kidney.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-3\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 3</p><h3>Impact of Graft Laterality on Surgical Complexity and Functional Outcomes Following Robot-assisted Kidney Transplantation: A High-volume Centre Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Kushwaha Swapnil Singh, Aggarwal Amit, Gaur Pankaj, Guleria Karandeep, Khan Umar, Kumar Anant</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPER SPECIALITY HOSPITAL, VAISHALI, GHAZIABAD, INDIA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>**Introduction &amp; Objectives:**<br/>\nRobot-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) is a safe, minimally invasive alternative to open transplantation; however, right-sided grafts (RSGs) pose technical challenges because of the shorter renal vein. Evidence regarding the effect of graft laterality on RAKT outcomes remains limited. We compared perioperative, surgical, and functional outcomes of right- versus left-sided living donor grafts at a high-volume robotic transplant centre.</p><p>**Materials &amp; Methods:**<br/>\nWe retrospectively analysed a prospectively maintained database of 428 consecutive RAKTs performed between 2016 and 2025. The cohort included 405 primary transplants in the right recipient bed (34 RSGs, 371 left-sided grafts [LSGs]) and 23 retransplants in the left recipient bed (4 RSGs, 19 LSGs). Donor and recipient characteristics, operative and ischemia parameters, complications, delayed graft function (DGF), and early and mid-term graft function were compared.</p><p>**Results:**<br/>\nBaseline characteristics were comparable. Mean console time was similar (208 ± 35 vs. 202 ± 38 minutes; P=0.35). RSGs had longer rewarm ischemia time (55.1 ± 16.2 vs. 48.6 ± 15.4 minutes; P=0.030), while warm (5.89 ± 1.91 vs. 5.51 ± 1.34 minutes; P=0.26) and cold ischemia times (47.2 ± 10.8 vs. 46.4 ± 11.3 minutes; P=0.68) were comparable. Serum creatinine on postoperative days 1, 5, and 7 did not differ significantly. DGF occurred in 1 RSG (3.2%) and 6 LSG recipients (2.4%; P=0.80). Major complications (Clavien–Dindo grade ≥III) were comparable (5.3% vs. 5.4%; P=0.40). On multivariable analysis, laterality independently predicted neither DGF nor major complications.</p><p>**Conclusions:**<br/>\nRSGs can be safely used for RAKT in experienced centres. Despite modestly prolonged rewarm ischemia, RSGs achieved surgical safety, early graft function, and complication rates comparable to LSGs without compromising clinically relevant outcomes. Graft laterality should not limit RAKT when appropriate expertise is available.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-4\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 4</p><h3>Virtual Anatomy, Real Surgical Impact: Three-Dimensional Model–Guided Robot-Assisted Partial Nephrectomy for Complex Hilar Renal Tumours—A Propensity-Matched Pilot Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Kushwaha Swapnil Singh, Aggarwal Amit, Gaur Pankaj, Chauhan Upwan Kumar, Dassi Vimal, Kumar Anant</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPER SPECIALITY HOSPITAL, VAISHALI, GHAZIABAD, INDIA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>**Introduction and Objectives:**<br/>\nRobot-assisted partial nephrectomy (RAPN) for complex hilar renal tumors is technically demanding because of their proximity to major vessels and the collecting system. Three-dimensional virtual models (3DVMs) reconstructed from conventional imaging may improve anatomical understanding, preoperative planning, and intraoperative decision-making. We evaluated our initial experience with 3DVM-guided RAPN for complex hilar tumors and compared perioperative, functional, and oncological outcomes with a propensity-matched cohort undergoing conventional RAPN.</p><p>**Methods:**<br/>\nTwenty-five non-consecutive patients with complex hilar renal tumors underwent 3DVM-guided RAPN at our institution. Patient-specific models were reconstructed from contrast-enhanced computed tomography images using UrologiQ (Biocliq Tech, India). Using propensity-score matching in a 1:2 ratio, these patients were compared with 50 controls selected from a database of 106 patients who underwent RAPN without 3DVM assistance between 2016 and 2025. Perioperative parameters, complications, renal function, and oncological outcomes were analyzed.</p><p>**Results:**<br/>\nAll procedures were performed transperitoneally using global hilar ischemia. The 3DVM group demonstrated significantly shorter console time (128±10 vs 142±12 minutes; P=0.01) and lower estimated blood loss (150±30 vs 250±35 mL; P=0.02). Warm ischemia time was comparable between groups (29±3 vs 31±4 minutes; P=0.25). No major complications (Clavien–Dindo grade ≥III) occurred, and length of hospital stay was similar. Negative surgical margins were achieved in all patients. Postoperative renal function, including renal function at three months, remained comparable between groups, without clinically meaningful deterioration.</p><p>**Conclusions:**<br/>\nPatient-specific 3DVMs improve anatomical orientation and may enhance surgeon confidence during RAPN for complex hilar tumors, while reducing console time and blood loss. However, when global ischemia is employed, these advantages do not translate into measurable improvements in warm ischemia time, postoperative renal function, complications, hospitalization, or early oncological outcomes. Larger prospectively designed studies with longer follow-up are required to define the clinical value and cost-effectiveness of 3DVM guidance, particularly for selective-clamping, super-selective, and zero-ischemia techniques.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-5\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 5</p><h3>Beyond Enucleation: Achieving the \"Trifecta\" in Robot-Assisted Simple Prostatectomy Through a Modified Surgical Technique</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Kushwaha Swapnil Singh, Aggarwal Amit, Gaur Pankaj, Chauhan Upwan Kumar, Dassi Vimal, Kumar Anant</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPER SPECIALITY HOSPITAL, VAISHALI, GHAZIABAD, INDIA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>OBJECTIVE:<br/>\nRobot-assisted simple prostatectomy (RASP) has emerged as a preferred modality for managing large volume benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). The concept of a “trifecta” outcome—defined as postoperative Qmax &gt;15 mL/s, IPSS &lt;8, and absence of major complications (Clavien–Dindo ≥3)—offers a comprehensive metric of surgical success. We present our experience with a modified RASP technique and evaluate trifecta outcomes in a high-volume cohort.<br/>\nPATIENTS AND SURGICAL PROCEDURE:<br/>\nA retrospective analysis of 94 consecutive patients undergoing RASP for large BPH was performed. Preoperative parameters included median age 68 years, prostate volume 164 cc, IPSS 27, Qmax 5.31 mL/s, and PVR 150 mL. Indications included refractory LUTS (85%) and failed TWOC (15%). A standardized modified transvesical approach emphasizing low anterior cystostomy and trigonization was employed. Perioperative outcomes, complications, and functional results at 3 months were analyzed.<br/>\nRESULTS:<br/>\nMedian operative time was 136 minutes with blood loss of 220 mL, and no patient required transfusion. Median hospital stay was 2 days, and catheter duration was 7 days. Complications were predominantly minor, including transient incontinence (11%) and fever (5.5%), with only one patient (1.8%) experiencing persistent stress urinary incontinence beyond 6 months. No major (Clavien ≥3) complications were observed. At 3 months, significant functional improvement was noted: IPSS decreased by 18 points to a median of 9, Qmax improved by +16.5 mL/s to 18.7 mL/s, and PVR reduced by 90 mL. 81% patients achieved trifecta outcomes, driven by excellent functional recovery and minimal morbidity.<br/>\nCONCLUSIONS:<br/>\nOur modified RASP technique demonstrates that trifecta outcomes are reproducibly achievable in large-volume BPH. The combination of meticulous surgical refinement and robotic precision translates into superior functional recovery with negligible major morbidity. This study reinforces RASP as not merely an alternative, but a benchmark procedure in the management of complex BPH, with trifecta outcomes serving as a indicator of excellence.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-6\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 6</p><h3>From Castleman to Constriction: salvaging a scarred ureter</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kapil, Dr Siddharth Yadav, Dr Dheerain, Dr Anup Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC and SJH, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS</p><p>To describe the surgical management of a challenging long-segment right ureteric stricture following previous retroperitoneal mass excision and radiotherapy for Castleman disease, highlighting the role of Boari flap reconstruction in complex distal ureteric defects.</p><p>METHODS</p><p>A 32-year-old male with a history of retroperitoneal Castleman disease underwent previous laparotomy and mass excision, followed by multiple interventions for ureteric injury and obstruction, including repeated DJ stenting and right percutaneous nephrostomy. Imaging demonstrated persistent right hydroureteronephrosis. Retrograde pyelography showed narrowing at L5–S1, while nephrostography demonstrated approximately 50% contrast retention at 20 minutes with no bladder opacification. Exploratory laparotomy was performed with excision of residual retroperitoneal lymph nodes and definitive ureteric reconstruction.</p><p>RESULTS</p><p>Dense adhesions between the right ureter and common iliac artery were encountered. Following meticulous ureterolysis, a nearly obliterative ~4-cm long-segment ureteric stricture with unhealthy mucosa was identified. The strictured segment was excised and the proximal ureter was inspected ureteroscopically. A psoas hitch was performed, followed by creation of an approximately 8-cm Boari flap from the anterior bladder wall. Ureteric reimplantation was completed over a 6 Fr × 26 cm DJ stent. A pelvic drain was placed and subsequently removed on postoperative day 5.</p><p>CONCLUSION</p><p>Complex ureteric strictures following retroperitoneal surgery and radiotherapy pose significant reconstructive challenges due to dense fibrosis, unhealthy tissue and loss of ureteric length. Boari flap with psoas hitch provides a versatile, durable option for reconstruction of long distal ureteric defects, allowing tension-free ureteroneocystostomy even in a hostile postoperative pelvis.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-7\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 7</p><h3>Giant 5-cm Calculus within a Periurethral Diverticulum in a female Patient: A Rare case</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR PAMPOSH RAINA, DR PAWAN KAUNDAL, DR MANJEET SHARMA, DR ARJUN SHARMA</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIMSS, CHAMIANA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Giant 5-cm Calculus within a Periurethral Diverticulum in a female Patient: A Rare case</p><p>Aims</p><p>To highlight the clinical presentation and diagnostic challenge in patients of calculus within a periurethral diverticulum and to describe its surgical management and clinical outcome.</p><p>Methods</p><p>A 56-year-old blind female patint presented with progressively worsening dysuria, urinary urgency, and increased urinary frequency for one year. MRI pelvis demonstrated a calcified periurethral lesion, with differential diagnoses of a calcified vaginal leiomyoma and calculus within a periurethral diverticulum. Cystourethroscopy was performed and revealed a giant calculus within a periurethral diverticulum. The patient subsequently underwent periurethral diverticulectomy with urethral repair and Martius flap interposition .</p><p>Results</p><p>Cystourethroscopy confirmed the presence of a calculus within the periurethral diverticulum. Complete diverticulectomy was performed with urethral reconstruction and Martius flap interposition. The postoperative period was uneventful. On follow-up, the patient demonstrated complete resolution of her symptoms.</p><p>Conclusions</p><p>Periurethral diverticular calculus is an uncommon cause of persistent lower urinary tract symptoms in women. A high index of suspicion is required when evaluating a calcified periurethral lesion. Cystourethroscopy can establish the diagnosis, while complete diverticulectomy with meticulous urethral repair and vascularized Martius flap interposition provides effective definitive treatment.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-8\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 8</p><h3>Outcomes of a Cost-Effective Pfannenstiel Incision for Graft Insertion in Robot-Assisted Kidney Transplantation: Does GelPoint™/Alexis™ Make a Difference?</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Kushwaha Swapnil Singh, Aggarwal Amit, Gaur Pankaj, Guleria Karandeep, Khan Umar, Mitra Shaunak, Kumar Anant</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPER SPECIALITY HOSPITAL, VAISHALI, GHAZIABAD, INDIA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Objectives:<br/>\nGraft insertion during robot-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) influences operative workflow, rewarming ischemia time, and graft outcomes. GelPoint™/Alexis™ permits graft introduction without interrupting robotic docking but substantially increases procedural cost. Pfannenstiel insertion is a simple, economical alternative requiring temporary undocking and re-docking. We compared perioperative, functional, and short-term outcomes of both techniques in a large single-centre RAKT series.</p><p>Materials and Methods:<br/>\nWe retrospectively reviewed a prospectively maintained database of 428 consecutive RAKTs performed between 2016 and 2025 at a high-volume robotic transplant centre. Grafts were inserted using GelPoint™/Alexis™ in 81 patients and a Pfannenstiel incision in 347. Ischemia times, insertion and docking time, postoperative renal function, delayed graft function (DGF), complications, and follow-up graft function were compared. Baseline characteristics were assessed to identify clinically relevant group differences. Renal function was evaluated using serial serum creatinine measurements during admission and at follow-up. Continuous and categorical outcomes were compared using appropriate statistical tests, with two-sided P values below 0.05 considered statistically significant. Technical feasibility and safety were also examined.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nWarm ischemia time (5.61±1.34 vs 5.78±1.49 minutes; P=0.42) and cold ischemia time (46.8±8.9 vs 47.5±9.2 minutes; P=0.67) were comparable between device and Pfannenstiel groups. Insertion and docking time was significantly shorter with the device (1.6±0.8 vs 9.4±2.7 minutes; P&lt;0.001), resulting in lower rewarming ischemia time (48.6±13.4 vs 57.2±15.6 minutes; P&lt;0.001). Nevertheless, serum creatinine on postoperative days 1, 5, and 7 was comparable. DGF occurred in 2 (2.5%) and 4 (1.2%) patients, respectively (P=0.48). At mean follow-up of 29.2±5.8 and 28.6±6.1 months, serum creatinine remained comparable (1.21±0.19 vs 1.24±0.22 mg/dL; P=0.52), with no difference in complications.</p><p>Conclusions:<br/>\nPfannenstiel graft insertion is a safe, reproducible, and cost-effective alternative to GelPoint™/Alexis™ in RAKT. Although it prolongs insertion and rewarming ischemia times, this did not compromise early or intermediate-term graft function or increase DGF or complications.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-9\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 9</p><h3>Artificial intelligence - driven predictive nomograms for safer percutaneous nephrolithotomy: enhancing early detection of postoperative infectious complications ans sepsis</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Raj singh chouhan , Dr Alok , Dr Apoorv saxena , Dr Arpan yadav , Dr Sanjoy sureka,</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">sanjay gandhi post graduate institute of medical sciences , lucknow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background Percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL) for renal stones is associated with infectious complications— fever (~ 20–30%), SIRS (~ 40%), and sepsis (&lt; 10%)—which can cause significant morbidity if not predicted early. Reliable risk-prediction tools may enable timely intervention. Objective To develop and internally validate logistic regression–based nomograms predicting postoperative fever, SIRS, and sepsis after PCNL. Methods We analyzed 800 adult patients undergoing PCNL (2020–2025). Comprehensive data was collected, including preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative variables. Univariate analysis was done and predictors significant for each model were evaluated further. Separate multivariable logistic regression models were constructed, converted into nomograms, and assessed using AUCs with 95% CIs, bootstrap validation, and calibration plots. Results Postoperative Procalcitonin(PCT) was the strongest independent predictor across outcomes (for sepsis: OR 1.21 per 1 ng/mL; 95% CI 1.06–1.38; p = 0.005). Larger stone size, alkaline urine pH, and positive urine culture were also significant, while operative variables were not. Incidence rates were 44.5% (fever), 27.0% (SIRS), and 8.1% (sepsis). Model discrimination was good, with AUCs of 0.82 for fever, 0.79 for SIRS, and 0.85 for sepsis. Bootstrap validation showed minimal optimism. Conclusion These internally validated nomograms demonstrate acceptable predictive performance for post-PCNL infectious complications and may provide practical tools for bedside risk stratification, supporting early intervention in high-risk patients and optimized resource allocation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-10\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 10</p><h3>Large Prostatic Abscess in an Immunocompromised Patient with Extrapelvic Gluteal Extension: Challenges in Diagnosis and Multimodal Management</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Qazi Imran, Dr Kunj Patel, Dr Amir Hussain</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">ERA MEDICAL COLLEGE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Prostatic abscesses are uncommon and may present atypically in immunocompromised patients. Extrapelvic extension into the gluteal region is rare and poses diagnostic and therapeutic challenges.</p><p>Case presentation: A middle-aged immunocompromised male presented with fever, perineal pain, and difficulty walking. Imaging revealed a large multiloculated prostatic abscess with extension into the left gluteal region through the ischiorectal fossa. Broad-spectrum antibiotics and image-guided aspiration failed to achieve resolution. The patient underwent combined transurethral deroofing and percutaneous gluteal drainage, resulting in marked clinical improvement and complete radiological resolution.</p><p>Discussion: Immunocompromised status predisposes to aggressive extension of prostatic abscesses. Multiloculated collections and extrapelvic spread often require multimodal drainage. Early imaging, tailored antibiotics, and choosing an appropriate surgical route are critical to avoid morbidity.</p><p>Conclusion: Extrapelvic extension of prostatic abscess represents a severe but treatable condition when managed with timely, combined surgical drainage.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-11\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 11</p><h3>Not Every Renal Agenesis is Zinner Syndrome: A Rare Mesonephric Duct Anomaly with Ipsilateral Testicular Hypoplasia</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Qazi Imran, Dr Kunj Patel, Dr Amir Hussain</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">ERA MEDICAL COLLEGE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction</p><p>Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct anomalies are rare congenital disorders affecting both the urinary and male reproductive tracts due to their common embryological origin. We report an uncommon variant presenting with unilateral renal agenesis and ipsilateral testicular hypoplasia without the classical features of Zinner syndrome.</p><p>Material and Methods</p><p>A 26-year-old man presented with psychogenic erectile dysfunction. He underwent clinical evaluation, semen analyses, hormonal profile, abdominal ultrasonography, pelvic MRI, and peripheral blood karyotyping to evaluate associated genitourinary anomalies and fertility status.</p><p>Results</p><p>Ultrasonography demonstrated left renal agenesis. Pelvic MRI revealed a markedly small left testis (3.4 cc) with normal seminal vesicles, prostate, and urinary bladder. Repeat semen analysis showed normal semen volume, sperm concentration (85 million/mL), motility (75%), and morphology (85% normal forms), indicating preserved fertility potential. Hormonal evaluation showed mildly elevated LH (13.89 IU/L) and FSH (10.22 IU/L) with high-normal testosterone (1189.95 ng/dL), consistent with compensated primary testicular dysfunction. Peripheral blood karyotyping revealed a normal male complement (46,XY). The patient was managed conservatively with counseling, and remained asymptomatic with satisfactory sexual function and stable findings on follow-up.</p><p>Conclusion</p><p>This case expands the spectrum of mesonephric duct anomalies and highlights the importance of comprehensive evaluation to differentiate atypical presentations from classical Zinner syndrome.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-12\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 12</p><h3>Adenocarcinoma over lateral wall of urinary bladder managed by robotic assisted partial cystectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Vikas Kumar Bansal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Yashoda hospital &amp; Research centre</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>The objective of this presentation was to demonstrate a case of adenocarcinoma over lateral wall of urinary bladder managed by robotic assisted partial cystectomy.<br/>\nA 60 year old diabetic hypertensive and chronic kidney disease gentleman presented to our hospital with complaints of intermittent painless hematuria. On routine evaluation he was found to have a large 3 cm growth over left lateral wall of urinary bladder with a raised serum creatinine of 6.5 mg/dl. The patient underwent TURBT which revealed a solitary large growth over left lateral wall of urinary bladder, the biopsy of which was adenocarcinoma (intestinal type) with deep muscle postive for tumor with FDG PET showing a localized disease. In view of the raised creatinine, patient was managed by robotic partial cystectomy. DJ stent was placed prior to proceeding with robotic to safeguard the left ureter.<br/>\nPatient was discharged on POD 4 with foley catheter<br/>\nHis biopsy revealed :Adenocarcinoma enteric variant,Grade 2 moderately differentiated,Invades muscularis propria<br/>\nAll margins negative with distance from invasive tumor to closest margin 5mm,Left side pelvic lymph nodes positive for tumor.<br/>\nFoley catheter removed after 3 weeks, DJ stent removed after 6 weeks and Referrred to medical oncologist and started on immunotherapy.<br/>\nPartial cystectomy is a feasible option in cases of primary adenocarcinoma of urinary bladder where patient is not fit for a radical cystectomy and ileal conduit<br/>\nWhile doing partial cystectomy, safeguarding the ureter while obtaining a negative margins are of utmost priority<br/>\nPreop U cath or DJ stent placement helps in identifying and safeguarding the ureter<br/>\nSome patient may require adjuvant therapies in cases of lymph node positive disease</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-13\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 13</p><h3>Early urodynamic evaluation in patients with posterior urethral valves: impact on renal outcomes and bladder function</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Devanshu Sharma, Dr Aniket Anand, Dr Anupam Shukla, Prof. M.S. Ansari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS, Lucknow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background and aims :<br/>\nBladder dysfunction associated with PUV can result in rapid renal deterioration. This study investigates the early urodynamic changes in PUV patients and assess whether early poor bladder function persists post TUVF to identify patients at risk progressing to chronic kidney disease (CKD).</p><p>Materials and methods:<br/>\nThis retrospective study analyzed 76 PUV patients within first two years of life following TUVF between 2010 and 2019 (excluding urinary diversion or additional surgeries).Patients were categorized into two groups: Group 1, characterized by early myogenic failure and Group 2, comprising the remaining patients. Urodynamic parameters, serum creatinine, estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR), and renal outcomes were assessed at baseline (3-months post-TUVF) anat three years post-TUVF. Results Group 1 exhibited lower baseline bladder capacity, compliance, and higher post-<br/>\nvoid residual volume compared to Group 2. At three years post-TUVF, Group 1 had a significantly lower eGFR, lower bladder compliance and higher rates of CKD. Anticholinergic use was higher in Group 1 (73% vs. 27%).</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nEarly urodynamic evaluation in PUV patients is crucial for understanding the last in impact on renal function, as early poor urodynamic features tend to persist and are associated with more adverse renal outcomes, despite intervention.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-14\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 14</p><h3>Augmented MRI-Based Radiomics for Accurate Subtype Classification of Renal Cell Carcinoma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Devanshu Sharma, Dr Apoorva Saxena, Dr Arpan Yadav, Dr Sanjoy Sureka</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS, Lucknow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS<br/>\nTo develop and evaluate an MRI-based radiomics framework enhanced by tailored image augmentation techniques for accurate non-invasive classification of renal cell carcinoma (RCC) subtypes</p><p>METHODS<br/>\nA retrospective radiomics study was conducted using T2-weighted MRI scans of 61 RCC patients obtained from The Cancer Imaging Archive (TCIA), comprising 46 ccRCC, 12 pRCC, and 3 chRCC cases. Tumors were manually segmented on the central slice using 3D Slicer. Following min-max normalization, 1,321 radiomic features were extracted using PyRadiomics. Dimensionality reduction using k-means clustering identified 22 key features for model development. Five machine learning classifiers—Support Vector Machine (SVM), Random Forest (RF), Logistic Regression (LR), Gradient Boosting (GB), and XGBoost (XGB)—were trained and optimized through grid-search hyperparameter tuning. Performance was evaluated using ROC-AUC, accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score.</p><p>RESULTS<br/>\nData augmentation expanded the dataset to 176 ccRCC, 252 pRCC, and 63 chRCC images. Among the evaluated classifiers, SVM demonstrated the highest diagnostic performance on the independent test dataset (n=32), achieving an AUC of 0.96, accuracy of 81%, precision of 89%, recall of 81%, and F1-score of 0.82. Random Forest achieved an AUC of 0.92, Logistic Regression 0.91, Gradient Boosting 0.90, and XGBoost 0.88. Five-fold cross-validation yielded high AUC values (0.93–0.99), indicating strong predictive capability, although potential overfitting was noted due to the limited original dataset size.</p><p>DISCUSSION<br/>\nThe findings demonstrate that augmentation-enhanced MRI radiomics can effectively capture intratumoral heterogeneity and improve subtype discrimination in RCC. Texture-based radiomic features emerged as important predictors, supporting previous evidence linking imaging heterogeneity to tumor biology. However, the small number of chromophobe RCC cases remains a limitation, and future studies should focus on multicenter validation, deep learning approaches, and federated learning frameworks to enhance generalisability.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-15\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 15</p><h3>Unexpected aggressiveness in VHL-associated clear cell renal cell carcinoma: insights from two patients highlighting biological heterogeneity</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Apoorva Saxena ,Dr Nitika Bharti, Dr Arpan Yadav, Dr Sanjoy Sureka</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS Lucknow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Objective:<br/>\nTo report two uncommon cases of aggressive clear cell renal cell carcinoma (ccRCC) in patients with suspected Von Hippel–Lindau (VHL) disease, highlighting the heterogeneity of tumour biology and the limitations of the conventional “3 cm rule” in the management of VHL-associated renal tumours.</p><p>Materials and Methods:<br/>\nTwo patients with clinically suspected VHL syndrome presenting with aggressive renal tumours were retrospectively reviewed. Clinical presentation, radiological findings, operative details, histopathology, and postoperative management were analysed.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe first patient, a middle-aged male, presented with bilateral renal masses, an inferior vena cava tumour thrombus, and synchronous liver metastases. He underwent right radical nephrectomy with inferior vena cava thrombectomy and contralateral nephron-sparing surgery. Histopathological examination revealed WHO/ISUP grade 2 clear cell RCC with metastatic disease. The second patient, a young male, presented with a large unilateral renal mass and underwent radical nephrectomy. Histopathology demonstrated WHO/ISUP grade 4 clear cell RCC with extensive sarcomatoid and rhabdoid differentiation, staged as pT3a. Despite the recognised indolent nature of most VHL-associated RCCs, both patients exhibited highly aggressive clinicopathological features, suggesting marked biological variability. Genetic evaluation for VHL syndrome is ongoing in both cases.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nThese cases challenge the traditional perception of uniformly indolent VHL-associated RCC and underscore the limitations of a size-based surveillance strategy alone. Early recognition of aggressive phenotypes through vigilant imaging, detailed histopathological assessment, and timely genetic evaluation is essential. Management should be individualised based on tumour biology and clinical behaviour to optimise oncological and renal functional outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-16\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 16</p><h3>Beyond the Chest: An Occult Adrenal Hematoma Following Blunt Thoracic Trauma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aayush Jain</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta- The Medicity, Gurugram, Haryana</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim-<br/>\nTo highlight an unexpected traumatic adrenal injury detected during evaluation of a patient presenting with isolated chest related symptoms after blunt thoracic trauma.</p><p>Methods-<br/>\nA 28-year-old man presented to Emergency Room after a road traffic accident in which he self-fell from a two-wheeler, sustaining direct impact to the chest. He complained of right-sided chest pain and dyspnea. There was no abdominal pain or distention. There was no history of loss of consciousness, seizures, or ENT bleed. There was also no history of alcohol, drug, or substance intoxication at the time of the incident. He was conscious, hemodynamically stable, and had no clinical features suggestive of ongoing abdominal/ thoracic hemorrhage. A contrast-enhanced whole-body trauma CT was performed as per institutional trauma protocol.</p><p>Results-<br/>\nContrary to the presenting symptoms, CT showed no significant intrathoracic injury. However, imaging unexpectedly demonstrated a 38 × 23 mm non-enhancing hyperdense hematoma involving the right adrenal gland with mild periadrenal fat stranding. There was no active contrast extravasation or pseudoaneurysm. No major associated intra-abdominal solid-organ injury was identified, and the contralateral adrenal gland was normal. A thin posterior interhemispheric subdural hematoma was noted.<br/>\nGiven the hemodynamic stability and absence of active adrenal bleeding, conservative management with observation, supportive care, and multidisciplinary consultation was undertaken. The patient improved clinically and was discharged in stable condition.</p><p>Conclusion-<br/>\nTraumatic adrenal hematoma is an uncommon and often clinically occult injury. This case presents a diagnostic paradox: symptoms and site of impact suggested thoracic trauma, whereas the significant traumatic finding was retroperitoneal. Careful evaluation of the entire trauma CT is therefore essential, as the site of injury may extend beyond the site of symptoms. In stable patients without active bleeding, conservative management can achieve a favorable outcome.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-17\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 17</p><h3>Surgical and Graft Outcomes in ABO-Incompatible Live-Donor Kidney Transplantation: A Five-Year Single-Tertiary-Centre Retrospective Analysis</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR Alok kumar,DR Apoorva Saxena,DR Sanchit Rustagi,DR Sanjoy Sureka,DR Uday pratap singh,DR MS Ansari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SANJAY GANDHI POSTGRADUATE INSTITUTE OF MEDICAL SCIENCES</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims-ABO-incompatible (ABOi) living-donor kidney transplantation has emerged as an important strategy to expand the donor pool and address the shortage of compatible living donors. Although immunological, infectious, graft, and patient survival outcomes have been extensively studied, data regarding perioperative and long-term surgical outcomes remain relatively limited. This study aimed to compare surgical, postoperative, graft, and patient outcomes between ABO-incompatible and ABO-compatible living-donor kidney transplantation.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective analysis was performed using a database of living-donor kidney transplant recipients treated at a single tertiary-care centre between 2018 and 2023. Patients undergoing ABO-incompatible transplantation were compared with ABO-compatible recipients matched in a 1:1 ratio using propensity scores. The primary outcome was the incidence of postoperative wound complications. Secondary outcomes included intraoperative blood loss, duration of drain placement, duration of double-J stenting, urinary leak, lymphocele, ureteric stricture, postoperative hospital stay, and graft and patient survival at 1 and 5 years.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nABO-incompatible transplantation was associated with less favourable short-term surgical outcomes compared with ABO-compatible transplantation, including increased intraoperative blood loss, a higher incidence of wound-related complications, greater and more prolonged postoperative drain output, and longer postoperative hospital stay. Graft and patient survival were slightly lower in the ABO-incompatible group; however, the difference was not statistically significant. Thus, despite increased perioperative morbidity, long-term graft and patient outcomes remained broadly comparable between the two groups.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nABO-incompatible living-donor kidney transplantation is associated with increased short-term surgical morbidity, particularly bleeding, wound complications, prolonged drain requirement, and longer hospitalization. Nevertheless, comparable long-term graft and patient survival suggest that ABO-incompatible transplantation remains a viable and effective option for patients with end-stage renal disease who lack an ABO-compatible living dono</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-18\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 18</p><h3>Bladder-Prostate Rhabdomyosarcoma in Children:Experience and Lessons from a Tertiary Urological Centre</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Mohd Azfar, Dr Apoorva Saxena, Dr Anupam Shukla, Dr Mohammed Suleh Ansari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sanjay Gandhi Postgraduate Institute of Medical Sciences</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims/Introduction: To review the contemporary management, clinical outcomes, and institutional experience of Pediatric bladder/Prostate rhabdomyosarcoma (BP-RMS) at a tertiary care center, highlighting diagnostic strategies, treatment modalities, and functional outcomes<br/>\n<br/>\n<br/>\nMaterials and Methods:<br/>\nA comprehensive retrospective analysis was performed on pediatric patients diagnosed with BP-RMS and treated at SGPGIMS, Lucknow. The study encompassed a detailed evaluation of clinical presentation, imaging findings, histopathological features, FOXO1 fusion status, staging workup, risk categorization, and adherence to treatment protocols. To further elucidate the decision-making process and illustrate the diversity of clinical scenarios, two representative cases were described in detail.<br/>\nDiagnostic assessment entailed the use of USG and CECT/MRI scans to characterize the tumor. All children underwent cystoscopic biopsy to confirm pathological diagnosis, and FOXO1 fusion testing to subtype the tumors. Metastatic staging included bone marrow aspiration, bone scan, and thoracic imaging to rule out distant spread. Risk stratification and subsequent treatment plans were implemented according to the Children’s Oncology Group (COG) risk-adapted protocols, integrating multi-agent chemotherapy (VAC regimen: vincristine, actinomycin, and cyclophosphamide), targeted radiotherapy, and selective use of surgical intervention based on tumor response and progression. Long-term follow-up emphasized regular cystoscopy, imaging assessments, and surveillance for late-onset complications.<br/>\n<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nBoth patients in the case series presented with lower urinary tract symptoms and varying degrees of bladder outlet obstruction, reflecting common initial manifestations of BP-RMS. The first case involved a patient with fusion-positive alveolar RMS, who responded favorably to combined chemoradiation, resulting in complete remission and preservation of bladder function—a key consideration in pediatric care to optimize quality of life. The second case featured a large embryonal RMS with suboptimal response to standard therapy, necessitating escalation to salvage chemotherapy. Due to persistent disease, the patient ultimately underwent robotic radical cystoprostatectomy with ileal conduit urinary diversion, exemplifying the need for individualized treatment in refractory cases.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-19\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 19</p><h3>Beyond Negative Nodes: Rapid Solitary In-Transit Metastasis After Partial Penectomy for Penile Squamous Cell Carcinoma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR MOHD AZFAR , DR ANIKET ANAND , DR AARYAN ANAND , DR SANCHIT RUSTAGI , DR UDAY PRATAP SINGH</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sanjay Gandhi Postgraduate Institute of Medical Sciences</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>*Aims and Objectives:* This case report aims to describe a rare instance of rapid solitary cutaneous recurrence in penile squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) despite negative nodal staging and clear surgical margins. It highlights diagnostic pitfalls in distinguishing benign lesions from malignancy and underscores the role of multidisciplinary management in achieving disease control.</p><p>*Methodology:* A male in his 50s presented with a recurrent penile lesion initially misdiagnosed as condyloma acuminatum via wedge biopsy. After lesion progression, repeat biopsy confirmed well-differentiated SCC (8 mm thickness, corpus spongiosum/lymphovascular invasion). Imaging (FDG-PET) revealed metabolic activity near the surgical stump. The patient underwent partial penectomy with 2 cm margins and bilateral robotic-assisted lymphadenectomy (0/5 nodes positive). Three months post-surgery, a pustule at the peno-pubic junction was biopsied, confirming residual SCC. Wide local excision with intraoperative frozen-section analysis and primary closure was performed.</p><p>*Results:* Initial misdiagnosis delayed definitive treatment by six months. Despite negative nodes and margins post-penectomy, a solitary cutaneous metastasis emerged at the peno-pubic junction within three months. Re-excision with frozen-section guidance achieved tumor-free margins. No adjuvant therapy was administered per guidelines, as the case lacked high-risk features (stage T2N0M0). Surveillance at three-month intervals showed no recurrence or distant metastasis.</p><p>*Conclusion:* This case illustrates the aggressive potential of penile SCC, emphasizing the need for repeat biopsies in persistent lesions and vigilance for early local recurrence even with favorable staging. Frozen-section analysis during re-excision was critical for complete resection. Multidisciplinary collaboration (urology, pathology, plastic surgery) optimized outcomes, supporting surgery and surveillance over adjuvant therapy in node-negative disease.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-20\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 20</p><h3>Recurrent Epididymo-orchitis and LUTS unveiling Zinner Syndrome with Giant Ureterocele and Ectopic Megaureter</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aayush Jain</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta- The Medicity, Gurugram, Haryana</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim-<br/>\nTo describe a rare case where investigation for recurrent epididymo-orchitis and obstructive LUTS lead to reveal rare diagnosis of Zinner Syndrome. Also lead to dilemma to treat every case with patients having Zinner syndrome.</p><p>Methods-<br/>\nA 25-year-old male presenting with right scrotal swelling, burning micturition, urinary frequency and poor urinary stream was thoroughly evaluated including clinical examination revealing palpable, thickened and tender right epididymis and on DRE cystic swelling palpable on right side. He had history of right ureterocele endoscopic laser incision and circumcision 3 years back. After investigations he subsequently underwent cystoscopy-guided incision of right ureterocele.</p><p>Results-<br/>\nInitial imaging demonstrated markedly atrophic right kidney with large intravesical cystic lesion measuring approximately 5.3 × 4.3 × 6.1 cm, communicating with tortuous blind-ending right ureter and extending into prostate, causing compression of prostatic urethra. Right seminal vesicle was bulky and cystically dilated. Left kidney was compensatory hypertrophied. Semen analysis demonstrated preserved sperm concentration and total sperm count. CBNAAT (TB -PCR) from right epididymis -negative.<br/>\nFollowing ureterocele incision, repeat CT urography demonstrated interval non-visualisation of ureterocele, but persistent ectopic insertion of right ureter with dilated tortuous megaureter. Right seminal vesicle and proximal vas deferens showed interval reduction in cystic dilatation. Uroflowmetry improved from maximum flow rate of 12 to 28 mL/s, with reduction in post-void residual urine from 108 to 12 mL. Cytospin smears from ureterocele fluid show predominantly blood with numerous (partially degenerated) spermatozoa, few urothelial cells, scattered bare nuclei and neutrophils.</p><p>Conclusion-<br/>\nThis case represents a rare and complex Wolffian duct anomaly- Zinner syndrome, with giant ureterocele and ectopic megaureter as prominent features. Presenting with recurrent epididymo-orchitis and obstructive LUTS. Recognition of associated renal and seminal tract abnormalities is essential for diagnosis and appropriate management.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-21\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 21</p><h3>Post-TURP thimble bladder with bilateral hydroureteronephrosis managed by robotic cystectomy and ileal conduit</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Jay Patel</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Fortis Hospital, Mohali</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo report a rare case of severe contracted “thimble bladder” with bilateral hydroureteronephrosis following TURP, highlighting a non-tuberculous etiology and definitive management.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 66-year-old man presented with persistent total urinary incontinence following TURP. His postoperative course was complicated by meatal stenosis and a false passage in the prostatic urethra, requiring suprapubic catheterization and bilateral percutaneous nephrostomies. Preoperative evaluation for genitourinary tuberculosis was negative. Cystoscopy, CT urography, operative findings and histopathology were reviewed.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nCystoscopy demonstrated an extremely small-capacity “thimble bladder.” CT urography showed a severely contracted bladder with marked perivesical stranding, bilateral hydroureteronephrosis and right ureteral kink at L3–L4, without calculi. Robotic-assisted simple cystectomy with ileal conduit diversion was performed. Intraoperatively, dense perivesical adhesions and fibrosis were noted, with a small, thick-walled bladder displaced into the posterior pelvis. Histopathology revealed chronic cystitis with stromal chronic inflammation, without granulomas or malignancy. The patient had an uneventful postoperative recovery, with a functioning stoma and stable renal function at follow-up.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nAlthough classically associated with genitourinary tuberculosis, thimble bladder may rarely result from severe chronic inflammation and bladder defunctionalization following TURP. In patients with persistent incontinence and upper tract dilatation after urethral surgery, early recognition of a contracted high-pressure bladder is essential. Robotic cystectomy with ileal conduit diversion provides definitive management in end-stage bladder fibrosis and may help preserve upper tract function.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-22\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 22</p><h3>Giant Ileal Neobladder Calculi with Concurrent Renal Staghorn Calculus: A Rare and Challenging Case</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Siddharth Sahu, Dr Mohit Sethi, Dr Naveen Bajiya, Dr Shivam Priyadarshi, Dr Govind Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS medical college and Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Calculus formation is a well-recognised late complication of orthotopic ileal neobladder diversion, driven by mucus retention, chronic bacteriuria, urinary stasis and a foreign-body nidus at the staple/suture line. Giant calculi (&gt;1 kg) are rare and typically reflect prolonged loss to urological follow-up. We report a giant neobladder calculus with a synchronous renal staghorn calculus, complicated intraoperatively by bowel injury.</p><p>Case Report: A 62-year-old male with a history of radical cystectomy and ileal orthotopic neobladder (2013) for high-grade urothelial carcinoma, and two prior episodes of Acute intestinal obstruction, presented with poor urinary stream, straining and right flank pain of one year's duration. Imaging (USG, CECT) revealed a right renal staghorn calculus (~53 mm) with hydroureteronephrosis and three giant calculi (66–75 mm) within the neobladder. He underwent open cystolithotomy, retrieving three calculi weighing 1.2 kg in total. Dense adhesions from prior surgery and AIO episodes resulted in two complete bowel transections and three partial perforations, necessitating intraoperative Gastrointestinal Surgery involvement, ileal resection and ileo-ileal anastomosis. The neobladder was closed in layers with suprapubic catheter and drain placement. Postoperative recovery was complicated by transient ileus and a surgical-site infection, both managed conservatively; the patient was discharged on postoperative day 14. The renal staghorn calculus was deferred for staged management.</p><p>Discussion &amp; Conclusion: Giant neobladder calculi, though uncommon, should be suspected in patients with orthotopic diversion presenting with voiding difficulty and irregular follow-up. Prior neobladder perforation predisposes to dense adhesions, substantially raising enterotomy risk during open cystolithotomy and warranting surgical readiness for concurrent bowel resection. Lifelong stone and metabolic surveillance remains essential after urinary diversion.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-23\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 23</p><h3>Consequences of Delayed VVF Repair: Psychosocial, Sexual, and Social Stigmatic Burden in a North Indian Public Health Setup</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Utkarsh Tyagi, Dr Prakhar Pratap,Dr Arpan Yadav</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS Lucknow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Objectives<br/>\nVesicovaginal Fistula (VVF) significantly impacts health indices &amp; QOL due to social discrimination and stigma. Timely repairs are crucial for reintegrating these women into the community. However, delays especially in public hospitals, often worsen overall health. This study aims to assess the psychosocial, sexual health, and social stigma in women with delayed surgical repair.</p><p>Materials and methods<br/>\nThis prospective, questionnaire-based cross-sectional study was conducted from January 2019 to December 2023 (n=48) on patients who underwent VVF repair. Patients with malignant fistulae and re-do fistulas were excluded. Validated and semi-structured questionnaires used for face-to-face interviews, including the WHOQOL-BREF, Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (HADS), Oslo Social Support Scale, Female Sexual Distress Scale-Revised (FSDS-R), and Stigma Scale for Chronic Illness Short Form (SSCI-8).</p><p>Results<br/>\nThe mean age (n=48) was 36 ± 5.1 years, with lower segment caesarean section (LSCS) as the most common etiology (43.75%). Patients were stratified into two groups: Group 1 (n=16, 33.3%) underwent repair within 6 months of primary surgery, and Group 2 (n=32, 66.7%) after 6 months. Group 1 had significantly higher overall QoL scores (40.2 ± 8.5) compared to Group 2 (33.5 ± 5.9, p = 0.0026). All WHOQOL-BREF domains (physical, psychological, social, and environmental) were significantly better in Group 1 (p &lt; 0.01 for all domains). Lower educational and financial status correlated with worse QoL (p&lt;0.05). Group 2 had higher rates of depression (71.87% vs 25%) and anxiety (81.2% vs. 43.7%, p&lt;0.05). Stigma and sexual distress were significantly higher in Group 2 (p&lt;0.001). Majority of the patients (93.7%) in Group 2 reported female sexual distress (FSD) as compared to Group 1 (31.3%).</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nDelayed VVF repair worsens psychosocial, sexual, and social outcomes. Prioritizing early intervention and holistic care is critical for patient reintegration</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-24\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 24</p><h3>Adult Wilms tumour with diffuse anaplasia: a rare diagnostic pitfall and therapeutic challenge</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Apoorva Saxena,Anupam Shukla,Devanshu Sharma,Mohd Sualeh Ansari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Adult Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma) is extraordinarily rare, comprising roughly 3% of all Wilms tumours and about 0.5% of adult renal malignancies. It mimics renal cell carcinoma (RCC), lacks a dedicated adult protocol and carries a poor prognosis. We report a monophasic blastemal adult Wilms tumour with diffuse anaplasia to highlight its diagnostic difficulty, high-risk histology and the decisive role of sustained treatment adherence.</p><p>Methods: A 20-year-old man presented with six months of right flank pain and painless gross haematuria. Contrast-enhanced CT showed a 10.5 × 8.0 cm heterogeneously enhancing right lower-pole renal mass abutting the IVC, with clear lung fields. He underwent open right radical nephrectomy with hilar node sampling, followed by histopathology, immunohistochemistry and stage-adapted multimodal therapy with longitudinal surveillance.</p><p>Results: Histology revealed a monophasic, purely blastemal tumour with diffuse anaplasia (Ki-67 ~70–80%), renal sinus fat and renal vein involvement, negative margins and 9/9 negative nodes; IHC was WT1 and CD99 positive with exclusion of RCC and other small round blue cell tumours — COG stage II, SIOP high-risk. After a treatment default, surveillance revealed pulmonary metastases (stage IV). High-risk multi-agent chemotherapy (vincristine, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, carboplatin, etoposide) with consolidation radiotherapy was complicated by febrile neutropenia. A further default preceded pulmonary relapse, salvaged with vincristine and dactinomycin to complete response. At last review (July 2026) he remained disease-free ~13 months after nephrectomy.</p><p>Conclusions: Adult Wilms tumour should enter the differential of any young adult with a large, rapidly enlarging renal mass. Diagnosis is histological, resting on blastemal morphology with WT1/CD99 positivity after excluding RCC. Diffuse-anaplastic, monophasic histology denotes high-risk disease; cure depends on a risk-adapted multimodal strategy and, critically, on sustained treatment adherence.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-25\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 25</p><h3>CO EXISTENCE OF SUPERIOR MESENTRIC ARTERY SYNDROME AND NUT CRACKER SYNDROME - A RARE ENTITY</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Saurabh Agrawal, Dr Smriti Agrawal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AG STONE HOSPITAL , LUCKNOW</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Nutcracker syndrome or left renal vein entrapment is a condition where left renal vein gets<br/>\ncompressed between superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and aorta. In normal anatomy, angle<br/>\nbetween superior mesenteric artery and aorta is atleast 40 o , however angle less than 40 o may<br/>\nlead to nutcracker syndrome. Nut cracker phenomenon is when patient is asymptomatic and<br/>\nnut cracker syndrome is when patient is symptomatic. Very rarely nut cracker syndrome is<br/>\nalso associated with Superior mesenteric artery syndrome when 3 rd part of duodenum gets<br/>\ncompressed by SMA and aorta [1]. Being uncommon, nutcracker syndrome and SMA<br/>\nsyndrome are often underdiagnosed and need a strong clinical suspicion. Symptoms can<br/>\ninclude intermittent hematuria, flank pain, proteinuria, pelvic pain, testicular varices in men<br/>\nleading to a clinical diagnosis confirmed by imaging studies. SMA syndrome usually present<br/>\nwith excessive flatulence, bilious vomiting and weight loss. This case highlights the<br/>\nuncommon presentation of both the syndromes in a young man.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-26\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 26</p><h3>Rescue Prostatic Artery Embolization for Recurrent Haematuria Following Prostatic Urethral Lift</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shubhankar Avasthi, Dr Pankaj Wadhwa, Dr HS Talwar, Dr CP Chauhan, Dr Rajesh Kumar Ahlawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super-speciality Hospital , Noida- 128</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Prostatic Urethral Lift (PUL) is considered a minimally invasive treatment for benign prostatic hyperplasia that mechanically retracts obstructing lobes, serving as an attractive option for high-risk surgical candidates. We report a rare case of recurrent, intermittent, gross arterial haemorrhage following PUL, successfully managed with salvage Prostatic Artery Embolization (PAE).<br/>\nCase Presentation: A multi-morbid 84-year male with severe coronary artery disease and chronic heart failure presented with refractory acute urinary retention of 6 months duration. He underwent an uncomplicated 5-implant PUL for 75 gm enlarged prostate; off antiplatelets. He had a successful trial without catheter (TWOC) and an unobstructed flow. 2 days later, following a Valsalva manoeuvre, he developed gross haematuria resulting in a 209 cc intravesical clot. His haemoglobin was 5.4 g/dl, necessitating packed red cell transfusions. Clot evacuation under LA revealed no active bleed. Repeat catheterization was done for a week, followed by an repeat TWOC. A similar episode ensued, managed in a similar manner. CT angiography suggested an arterial blush on the left side of the prostate in the vicinity of an implant; the implants were in position. His prohibitive surgical risk, recurrent bleeding necessitated an alternative plan.<br/>\nManagement: A conventional angiogram confirmed the arterial blush, a successful selective PAE was performed.<br/>\nOutcomes: The endovascular procedure yielded durable haemostasis without peri-procedural complications. The patient passed a trial without catheter two weeks post-procedure and safely resumed his antiplatelet therapy. At the five-month follow-up, he remained asymptomatic with excellent voiding parameters, recording a maximum flow rate of 22 ml/sec.<br/>\nConclusion: While PUL carries a favourable safety record, severe haemorrhage can rarely occur. This case highlights PAE as a safe and definitive rescue modality for severe prostatic bleeding in medically frail patients who are unfit for traditional surgery.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-27\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 27</p><h3>The Litho-VR Trial : Beyond Analgesia Evaluating the Impact of Immersive Virtual Reality on Patient Comfort and Procedural Efficiency in ESWL</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Manish Kumar, Dr Kawaljit Singh Kaura, Dr Suresh Goyal, Dr Abhay S Gaur</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, BATHINDA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo evaluate the impact of immersive Virtual Reality (VR) distraction on intra-procedural pain, anxiety, procedure duration, fluoroscopy screening time, and opioid analgesia requirements during Extracorporeal Shockwave Lithotripsy (ESWL).</p><p>Methods<br/>\nIn a prospective randomized controlled trial, 100 adult patients with radiopaque renal or upper ureteric calculi undergoing ESWL were randomized into Group 1 (VR group, n=50), receiving head-mounted VR distraction, and Group 2 (Control group, n=50), receiving standard care. Baseline stone parameters and procedural variables were recorded. Primary outcomes were post-procedural Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) scores for pain and anxiety, alongside rescue IV Tramadol. Secondary outcomes included procedure duration, fluoroscopy time, and Clavien-Dindo complications.</p><p>Results<br/>\nBaseline stone size (12.62±2.68 vs 13.70±4.62 mm; p=0.156), density (1076.20±244.49 vs 1032.05±244.27 HU; p=0.369), shock waves (3905.90±556.54 vs 3889.71±536.63; p=0.883), and energy delivered (82.96±17.30 vs 83.09±20.20 J; p=0.973) were comparable. The VR group demonstrated statistically significant reductions in pain scores (VAS 3.14±2.61 vs 4.67±2.54; p=0.0037) and anxiety scores (VAS 1.43±1.80 vs 2.71±2.31; p=0.0026). Statistically significant reductions were also demonstrated in total procedure duration (72.53±16.33 vs 82.85±19.68 min; p=0.0053) and total fluoroscopy screening time (4.56±1.59 vs 6.21±2.21 min; p&lt;0.0001) in favor of the VR group. Rescue Tramadol requirements were significantly lower with VR (20.0% vs 42.0%; p=0.0306). Minor complications (Clavien-Dindo Grade I) were similar (10.0% vs 14.0%; p=0.758). No VR-related adverse events occurred.</p><p>Conclusions<br/>\nImmersive VR is an effective, safe non-pharmacological adjunct during ESWL. Beyond significantly reducing pain and anxiety, VR achieves statistically significant reductions in fluoroscopy radiation exposure and overall procedure duration by enhancing patient stillness and compliance.<br/>\nKeywords: Shockwave Lithotripsy, Virtual Reality, Distraction Analgesia, ESWL, RSD</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-28\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 28</p><h3>Overcoming the median lobe challenge in minimally invasive treatment of Benign Prostatic Enlargement: A hybrid approach with limited median lobe resection.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Puneet Sharma, Ritesh Goel, Karandeep Guleria, Mrigank S Jha, M Umar Khan Sherwani,</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max super speciality hospital saket</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims</p><p>To evaluate the functional outcomes of a hybrid approach combining minimally invasive therapy with limited median-lobe resection using Holmium laser for men with lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) with benign prostatic enlargement (BPE) and a median lobe while preserving antegrade ejaculation.</p><p>Methods</p><p>4 men underwent a hybrid procedure with limited median-lobe resection using Holmium laser. 3 patients underwent prostatic urethral lift procedure and 1 patient underwent Rezūm. Mean-age, prostate volume and median-lobe size were noted. IPSS, IPSS-QoL, maximum flow rate (Q-max) and post-void residual (PVR) were assessed preoperatively and at 1-month follow-up. Early postoperative complications and ejaculatory function were assessed. Pre- and postoperative functional parameters were compared using a paired Student's t-test.</p><p>Results</p><p>The mean age was 54.0 ± 3.2 years, mean prostate volume 45.2 ± 9.7 mL, and mean median-lobe size 14.2 ± 7.8 mm. Mean IPSS decreased from 23.25 ± 4.57 to 5.00 ± 0.82 (p=0.0063), while IPSS-QoL improved from 4.75 ± 0.96 to 1.00 ± 0.00 (p=0.0043). Mean Qmax increased from 5.20 ± 2.81 ml/sec to 27.00 ± 5.72 mL/s (p=0.0047), and PVR</p><p>decreased from 382.5 ± 155.9ml to 14.5 ± 4.4 mL (p=0.0189). Antegrade ejaculation was preserved in all four patients (100%). No postoperative urinary retention, UTI or readmission was recorded.</p><p>Conclusions</p><p>Early observation suggests that combining minimally invasive treatment of BPE with limited median-lobe resection using Holmium laser can provide substantial improvement in LUTS, quality of life, urinary flow and bladder emptying while preserving ejaculation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-29\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 29</p><h3>Giant Oncocytic Adrenal Neoplasm: Radiological Suspicion of Malignancy Versus Histopathological Reality</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aayush Jain</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta- The Medicity, Gurugram, Haryana</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim-<br/>\nWith increasing availability of imaging and minimal access surgery, incidence of adrenal incidentalomas increasing. With availability of IHC markers, Histopathological surprises are not uncommon. To highlight diagnostic and surgical challenges posed by giant oncocytic adrenal neoplasm with radiological features suggestive of adrenocortical malignancy.</p><p>Methods-<br/>\nA 61-year-old man presented with large left adrenal mass detected on imaging. He underwent CECT, FDG PET-CT, and full hormonal workup. Imaging revealed large heterogeneous suprarenal mass measuring approximately 13 × 12 × 14cm with central necrosis, irregular enhancement, and intense FDG uptake (SUVmax 61). Multiple enlarged para-aortic lymph nodes present, with apparent loss of fat planes with adjacent structures including pancreas, spleen, left renal cortex, and left renal vein, raising suspicion of adrenocortical carcinoma. Endocrine assessment showed no biochemical evidence of hormonal excess. After tumour board discussion, patient underwent open left adrenalectomy with en bloc left simple nephrectomy and retroperitoneal lymph node dissection.</p><p>Results-<br/>\nIntraoperatively, large mass measuring approximately 20 × 15 × 15cm was identified, densely adherent to surrounding structures including pancreas and left diaphragmatic crus, but without peritoneal dissemination or ascites. Hemodynamic parameters remained stable during tumour manipulation. Histopathological examination revealed oncocytic adrenal neoplasm measuring 15 × 10 × 9cm, confined to adrenal cortex. There was no capsular, vascular, or lymphatic invasion. Surgical margins were negative, and all nine retrieved lymph nodes were free of tumour (pT2N0). Mitotic activity was low (2/50HPF), with Ki-67 index of 8–10%. Helsinki score was 15, supporting low malignant potential. At 6-month follow-up, imaging showed no evidence of recurrence or residual disease.</p><p>Conclusion-<br/>\nGiant oncocytic adrenal neoplasms may closely mimic adrenocortical carcinoma on imaging due to their size, necrosis, and high FDG uptake. Definitive diagnosis relies on histopathological evaluation and validated scoring systems, essential for accurate staging, prognostication, and long-term postoperative surveillance.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-30\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 30</p><h3>Our Initial Experience with Living Donor Renal Transplantation at a Tertiary Care Centre: A Series of 15 Donor Nephrectomies</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kajari Ashtaputre, Dr Nishikant, Dr Gunjan, Dr Uma Kant Dutt</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIIMS BILASPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nLiving donor renal transplantation is an established treatment for end-stage kidney disease. Establishing a transplant programme at a newly developing tertiary care centre requires meticulous donor evaluation and coordinated multidisciplinary care. We present our initial experience with living donor nephrectomy and renal transplantation.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nWe retrospectively analysed 15 consecutive living donor nephrectomies. Donor demographics, donor–recipient relationship, comorbidities, renal function, CT renal angiography, laterality, surgical approach, operative time, and recipient outcomes were evaluated. All donors underwent clinical, biochemical and functional assessment, including DTPA renography and CT renal angiography.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nFifteen donor nephrectomies were performed. Donor age ranged from 22–64 years; three had hypertension and three were chronic smokers. Mean donor serum creatinine at Preop 0.67 mg/dL and at 3-month follow-up was 0.94 mg/dL. DTPA showed preserved bilateral renal function with non-obstructive drainage. Mean split function of the donated kidney was 46.9%, with mean GFR of 43.6 mL/min. CT angiography identified pre-hilar branching in 8 cases (mean arterial length, 1.7 cm), Late venous confluence in 3 cases (mean length, 1.3 cm from lateral border of Aorta), accessory renal arteries in 2 cases and duplicated collecting systems in 2 cases. Mean renal artery diameter was 4.6 mm. Left nephrectomy was performed in 13 (86.7%) and right nephrectomy in 2 (13.3%). All procedures used a laparoscopic-assisted open approach; mean operative time was 3 hours. One donor developed paralytic ileus, managed conservatively. One recipient required two separate arterial anastomoses for a graft with two renal arteries. All 15 transplants were successfully completed, with Mean serum creatinine of recipient is 1.1 mg/dl over 3 month follow up and no graft failure or mortality.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nOur initial experience demonstrates the feasibility of establishing a living renal transplantation programme. Detailed functional and vascular assessment enabled safe management of anatomical variations and complex renal vasculature, with excellent outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-31\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 31</p><h3>Penile Fracture: A Single-Centre Experience of Clinical Presentation, Surgical Management, and Short-Term Outcomes</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kajri Ashtaputre, Dr Nishikant, Dr Gunjan, Dr Uma Kant Dutt</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Aiims, Bilaspur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Penile fracture is an uncommon urological emergency that generally requires prompt surgical treatment. Although diagnosis is often clinical, presentations vary, and the classical “eggplant” deformity may be absent. We reviewed our institutional experience to describe clinical presentation, injury characteristics, surgical management, and early functional outcomes.</p><p>Methods: We retrospectively reviewed patients who underwent surgical exploration and repair for penile fracture at our centre. Data on age, mechanism, time to presentation, clinical findings, imaging, timing of surgery, site and size of tunical tear, urethral involvement, and postoperative outcomes were collected. Patients were followed for three months, with particular attention to erectile function.</p><p>Results: Eight patients were included, with a mean age of 40.6 years (range, 19–60 years). Sexual activity caused the injury in seven patients (87.5%), while one (12.5%) sustained penile trauma from a metallic foreign body. Five patients (62.5%) presented within 24 hours and three (37.5%) later. The classical eggplant deformity was present in only four patients (50%). Hematuria occurred in one patient. Ultrasonography demonstrated a tunical defect or suspicious defect with associated hematoma. Four patients (50%) underwent exploration within 24 hours, while four underwent surgery later. Tunical tears measured 5 mm–2 cm and were predominantly ventral, involving the mid or distal shaft, often near the urethra. No urethral injuries were identified. One patient had a dorsal mid-shaft defect with a retained metallic foreign body sustained at an iron mill. At three-month follow-up, none reported erectile dysfunction.</p><p>Conclusion: Penile fracture may present without the classical eggplant deformity. Despite the small series, surgical exploration and repair produced favourable short-term functional outcomes, with no erectile dysfunction during follow-up. A high index of suspicion is essential when classical features are absent to facilitate timely diagnosis and treatment.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-32\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 32</p><h3>Outcomes of Single-Vessel vs Multiple-Vessel Grafts in Robot-Assisted Kidney Transplantation at a High- Volume Tertiary Hospital</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Nipun Bansal, Swapnil Kuswaha, Amit Aggarwal, Pankaj Gaur, Upwan Chauhan, Vimal Dassi, Anant Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super Speciality Hospital, Vaishali, Ghaziabad (NCR Delhi)</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Multiple vessels in donor kidney poses technical challenges in robot-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT). This study aims to compare the surgical and functional outcomes between single vessel and multiple vessel grafts in robotic kidney transplants performed at our high-volume center.<br/>\nMaterial and Methods: A retrospective analysis of 259 robot-assisted kidney transplants (2018–2024) compared single-vessel grafts (Group A, n=203) with multivessel grafts (Group B, n=56). Efforts were made to combine multiple arteries into single vessel for easy anastomoses. Demographics, donor profiles, ischemia times, reconstruction techniques, and postoperative creatinine levels (POD-1, 7, 30, 90) were evaluated.<br/>\nResults: Cold ischemia time was higher in Group B (91.78 ± 58.04 mins) than Group A (68.01 ± 22.25 mins), though not statistically significant (p = 0.0639). Multiple vessel grafts required reconstruction: pantaloon (n=41), end-to-side (n=8), separate implantation (n=2), and internal iliac artery graft (n=5). However, re-warm ischemia times, postoperative outcomes and short-term graft function remained equivalent between groups.<br/>\nConclusion: RAKT with multiple-vessel grafts is feasible and yields results as good as those with single vessel cases. Multiple vessels should be converted into single vascular stump to minimize the rewarm ischemia time. Cold ischemia time may be longer in multiple vessel cases due to additional reconstruction steps, but this does not translate into inferior short-term graft function.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-33\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 33</p><h3>Beyond the karyotype: Complete Androgen insensitivity Syndrome: Management, Medico-legal and Multi-disciplinary perspectives</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kapil Nathani, Dr Biswajeet Sarmah, Dr Aravind TK, Dr Siddharth Yadav, Dr Anup Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC and SJH, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS</p><p>To present the diagnostic evaluation and management of a 19-year-old phenotypic female with Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS) presenting with primary amenorrhoea and to highlight the importance of multidisciplinary management including the surgical, endocrine, psychological and medico-legal aspects of CAIS.</p><p>METHODS</p><p>A 19-year-old phenotypic female presented with primary amenorrhoea, normal breast development and sparse pubic and axillary hair. Examination revealed female external genitalia and a short, blind-ending vaginal pouch. Hormonal evaluation demonstrated elevated testosterone (4.95 ng/mL) and LH (38.63 mIU/mL), with normal FSH, estradiol and prolactin. Karyotyping revealed a 46,XY pattern. MRI pelvis demonstrated bilateral undescended intra-abdominal gonads extending into the inguinal canals, with absence of uterus, fallopian tubes and ovaries. Diagnostic laparoscopy was performed, confirming bilateral testes and absence of Müllerian structures, followed by bilateral laparoscopic gonadectomy. Histopathological examination was performed on both specimens. The case was evaluated in the context of contemporary multidisciplinary and medico-legal principles of DSD management.</p><p>RESULTS</p><p>The clinical, biochemical, radiological and operative findings established the diagnosis of CAIS. Laparoscopy identified bilateral gonads anteromedial to the external iliac veins near the deep inguinal rings, with bilateral vas deferens and no uterus or fallopian tubes. Bilateral gonadectomy was completed without reported perioperative complications, and the patient was discharged on postoperative day one. Histopathology demonstrated bilateral testicular tissue with Leydig cell hyperplasia and no malignancy. Post-gonadectomy management requires lifelong oestrogen replacement, bone-health surveillance and consideration of vaginal rehabilitation, with ongoing psychological and multidisciplinary support.</p><p>CONCLUSION</p><p>CAIS should be considered in phenotypic females presenting with primary amenorrhoea, well-developed breasts, sparse pubic hair and absent uterus. A combination of hormonal assessment, karyotyping and anatomical imaging facilitates diagnosis and operative planning. Post-pubertal gonadectomy, followed by appropriate hormone replacement and long-term multidisciplinary follow-up. Sensitive disclosure, informed consent and attention to the evolving medico-legal framework are integral to management</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-34\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 34</p><h3>Cystitis Cystica Presenting as a Flap Valve Causing Bladder Outlet Obstruction: A Rare and Deceptive Mechanism</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Shikhar Garg, Dr.Ankur Mittal, Dr Vikas Kumar Panwar, Dr.Nischay Gupta</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Rishikesh</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background<br/>\nCystitis Cystica (CC) is a benign reactive proliferative disorder of the bladder urothelium arising from<br/>\nchronic inflammatory stimuli. . While predominantly an<br/>\nirritative finding, its capacity to mechanically obstruct the lower urinary tract is exceedingly rare and<br/>\nclinically underappreciated.<br/>\nCase<br/>\nA 52-year-old male presented with 2 years of LUTS (IPSS 14/35) dominated by intermittency.<br/>\nUroflometry shows a irregular intermittent saw-tooth pattern with reduced average flow rate and<br/>\ncompressive curve at low volume; suggestive of dynamic obstruction-PBNO type. CT-Urogram<br/>\nshowed a 1×1.8 cm trigonal cyst near the right VUJ. Intra-operatively, a 2×1 cm right lateral bladder-<br/>\nneck cyst acting as a dynamic flap valve was identified, along with multifocal trigonal and posterior-<br/>\nwall cysts. TUR was performed, followed by BNI at 7 o'clock from 1 cm distal to the right ureteric<br/>\norifice to the verumontanum. Histopathology of the cyst confirmed Cystitis Cystica. Postop<br/>\nuroflowmetry was normal. Patient was followed up for 6 months and is doing well.<br/>\nDiscussion<br/>\nThe flap-valve mechanism in this case is due to intermittent dynamic prolapse of a peri-bladderneck<br/>\ncyst over the internal urethral orifice during voiding explains the predominant symptom of<br/>\nintermittency; an unusual presentation and not well described in existing literature. Only sparse<br/>\nliterature exists describing cystitis cystica causing BOO, with most reported cases involving fixed<br/>\nobstruction due to bulky trigonal or peri-ureteric lesions. It also highlights the importance of direct<br/>\nendoscopic evaluation in cases with discordant or atypical urodynamic findings and atypical<br/>\npresentation of cystitis cystica<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nCC at the bladder outlet can produce a dynamic flap-valve mechanism causing BOO . TUR with BNI offers definitive surgical relief. Surveillance cystoscopy is recommended given risk of<br/>\nrecurrence and rare malignant transformation.<br/>\nKeywords-Cystitis Cystica · Bladder Outlet Obstruction · Flap Valve · LUTS · Transurethral<br/>\nResection · BNI</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-35\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 35</p><h3>An Unusual Culprit on Surveillance Imaging: Local Recurrence Within the Ileal Conduit After Radical Cystectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sahil mahajan, Dr Saroj k yadav, Dr Boris K, Dr Rudra Prasad ghorai, Dr Sidhartha Kalra, Dr Parthiban palanivel, Dr Sreerag KS</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">JIPMER, PUDUCHERRY</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nThe ileal conduit is the commonest urinary diversion after radical cystectomy. Two malignant processes may involve it: rare de novo secondary intestinal tumours (nitrosamine-driven; incidence ~0.02%, mean latency ~22 years) and, far more rarely, recurrence of the original urothelial carcinoma at the conduit or uretero-ileal anastomosis from field-cancerisation of retained urothelium. Both present with stomal bleeding and positive cytology, underscoring EAU recommendations for periodic upper-tract imaging after cystectomy. We report such a urothelial recurrence and its management.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nA 62-year-old woman underwent open radical cystectomy with bilateral pelvic lymph node dissection and ileal conduit diversion in April 2024 for muscle-invasive bladder cancer. Histopathology showed high-grade papillary urothelial carcinoma with deep muscle and lymphovascular invasion, negative nodes (0/8) and clear ureteral, urethral and vaginal-cuff margins (pT2bN0Mx). On surveillance CT urography, an enhancing soft-tissue lesion (4.2 × 2.6 × 2.7 cm) was identified at the uretero-ileal anastomosis with distal right ureteric thickening, consistent with recurrence, alongside gross right hydroureteronephrosis and a non-excreting right kidney; the left renal unit drained normally. Given isolated recurrence with a non-functioning ipsilateral kidney and previously clear ureteric margins (true metachronous recurrence), she underwent open right radical nephroureterectomy with en bloc ileal conduit excision, intraoperative frozen-section of the left ureteric margin, and left cutaneous ureterostomy.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nThe procedure was completed successfully. The left ureteric frozen-section margin was clear, permitting cutaneous ureterostomy.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nUrothelial recurrence at the conduit/uretero-ileal is a rare but important surveillance finding after cystectomy, demanding a high index of suspicion given the poor prognosis of post-cystectomy upper-tract recurrence. With a non-functioning ipsilateral kidney, radical nephroureterectomy with en bloc conduit excision and contralateral cutaneous ureterostomy provides effective locoregional control with durable diversion.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-36\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 36</p><h3>Torsion testes salvaged by longitudinal incision of tunica albuginea and tunica vaginalis graft/ flap</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Gaurav Jain, Dr. Taranpreet Singh, Dr. Shubham Jain, Dr. Mukesh Arya, Dr. Ajay Gandhi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nSome patients with torsion testes present late (12-24 hours) with questionable viability. In such cases, despite detorsion, further damage occurs by high intratesticular pressure and compartment syndrome. In these cases, a longitudinal incision on tunica albuginea and a tunica vaginalis flap/ graft improves vascularity abruptly (fig 1). We present a series of 20 such cases.<br/>\nMaterial and methods:<br/>\nThe cohort, consisting of 20 patients aged 3 to 12 years, presented with acute testicular torsion at 12-24 hours and underwent surgery. Follow-up assessments were conducted using color Doppler ultrasonography over a 6-month period to monitor testicular viability and size.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nResults demonstrated successful testicular salvage in 100% of cases but 85% of patients exhibited a decrease in testicular size (10-15%) on follow-up imaging.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nThose with testicular torsion presenting late (12-24 hours) can still be salvaged with this technique with 10-15% loss of volume. To further validate the data, we need to do testicular biopsy and testicular vein sampling (to measure testosterone levels) when they achieve puberty.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-37\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 37</p><h3>Superior Vesical Fissure in a 16-Year-Old Male: A Rare Variant of Bladder Exstrophy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Shubham Jain, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Taranpreet Singh, Dr. Mukesh Arya</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>INTRODUCTION<br/>\nSuperior vesical fissure is an exceptionally rare and mild variant of bladder exstrophy, characterized by a defect in the bladder dome with otherwise preserved bladder and urethral anatomy. The musculature and skeletal abnormalities resemble those of classic bladder exstrophy, while bladder extrusion is minimal and typically occurs around the umbilical region. Recognition of this entity is important, particularly in patients presenting late with urinary leakage.<br/>\nCASE PRESENTATION<br/>\nA 16-year-old male presented with lifelong urinary leakage from a small opening just below the umbilicus, occurring only during voiding. Examination revealed a low-set umbilicus with a fistulous opening and normal external genitalia. Pelvic radiography demonstrated pubic diastasis. Micturating cystourethrogram showed a normal bladder and urethra with contrast leakage from the bladder dome, without vesicoureteral reflux. The upper urinary tracts were normal. Cystoscopy confirmed the diagnosis of superior vesical fissure.<br/>\nMANAGEMENT<br/>\nFollowing delineation of the anatomical defect, surgical closure of the fissure was performed in multiple layers. A circumferential elliptical incision was made around the fistulous opening, followed by careful dissection to delineate the tract and expose the bladder dome. The bladder mucosa and detrusor were closed separately, with an additional seromuscular layer to achieve a watertight closure.<br/>\nCONCLUSION<br/>\nSuperior vesical fissure represents a rare, mild form of bladder exstrophy that may remain undiagnosed until adolescence or adulthood. A low-set umbilicus with pubic diastasis and urinary leakage from a suprapubic opening should raise suspicion for this anomaly and distinguish it from a patent urachus. Appropriate imaging and cystoscopy facilitate diagnosis, while surgical closure offers definitive treatment with a generally favorable prognosis.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-38\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 38</p><h3>Posterior sagittal repair of rectourethral fistula in a young male post – hemorrhoidectomy: A case report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Taranpreet Singh, Dr. Mukesh Arya, Dr. Yogendra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nRectourethral fistula (RUF) is an uncommon condition with diverse etiologies and clinical presentations. A universally accepted surgical technique for its repair has yet to be established. Acquired RUFs are most frequently associated with multimodal prostate cancer treatment, particularly involving radiation [2,3]. Non-radiation-related causes include surgical procedures (65%), trauma (22%), and inflammatory bowel disease (6%) [1]. Conservative management typically involves urinary diversion (UD) with or without fecal diversion (FD), but spontaneous healing occurs in only about 10% of cases [1].<br/>\nSeveral surgical techniques for RUF repair exist, all centered on fundamental principles such as excision of the fistulous tract, debridement, and tissue interposition to separate the rectum and urethra [4].</p><p>Objectives:<br/>\nThis case report aims to present our experience in managing a rare case of post-hemorrhoidectomy RUF using a posterior sagittal approach, which led to a successful outcome.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA 35-year-old male developed a rectourethral fistula following a hemorrhoidectomy. Initial management involved both fecal and urinary diversion. After three months, definitive surgical repair was performed using the posterior sagittal approach. Subsequently, both diversions were reversed.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe patient had an uneventful recovery. Postoperative imaging confirmed the successful closure of the fistula.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nThe posterior sagittal approach offers a feasible and effective alternative for repairing rectourethral fistulas in select, rare cases such as post-hemorrhoidectomy complications.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-39\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 39</p><h3>Primary Urethral Melanoma in a Female: A Rare Case with Local Recurrence After Initial Wide Local Excision</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Taranpreet Singh, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Shubham Jain, Dr. Mukesh Arya, Dr. Ajay Gandhi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIM<br/>\nPrimary urethral melanoma is an extremely rare malignancy, accounting for approximately 4% of urethral neoplasms and occurring more commonly in females. Its rarity, aggressive biological behaviour, large tumour size, high propensity for recurrence, and lack of consensus regarding staging and optimal surgical management make it a challenging entity. We present a rare case of primary female urethral melanoma managed initially with wide local excision, with subsequent recurrence.<br/>\nMATERIAL AND METHODS<br/>\nA 51-year-old female presented with an approximately 3.5 cm vulval mass attached to the urethral meatus, with bilateral insignificant inguinal lymph nodes. PET-CT demonstrated an FDG-avid 3.5 × 3.8 × 2.8 cm mass adjacent to the urethral meatus, with no evidence of FDG-avid disease elsewhere. The patient underwent wide local excision with partial urethrectomy under spinal anaesthesia.<br/>\nRESULTS<br/>\nHistopathological examination revealed spindle-cell malignant melanoma, with immunohistochemistry showing positivity for S-100 and HMB-45 and tumour-free surgical margins. The patient was referred for further oncological management and subsequently received systemic chemotherapy with dacarbazine and cisplatin. Despite initial surgical management, the patient developed local recurrence, highlighting the aggressive nature of primary urethral melanoma and the challenges in achieving durable local control.<br/>\nCONCLUSION<br/>\nPrimary female urethral melanoma is an exceptionally rare and aggressive malignancy, with only a limited number of cases reported in the literature. Wide local excision may provide initial local control; however, recurrence remains a significant concern. The optimal extent of urethral resection, role of radiotherapy, systemic chemotherapy and newer immunotherapeutic strategies remains uncertain. Long-term surveillance and a multidisciplinary approach are essential in view of the aggressive behaviour and high recurrence potential of this uncommon malignancy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-40\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 40</p><h3>Preputioplasty in Hypospadias: A Series of 58 Cases</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Gaurav Jain, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Shubham Jain, Dr. Mukesh Arya, Dr. Yogendra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIM<br/>\nTo evaluate the feasibility and outcomes of preputial reconstruction in patients with hypospadias in whom the prepuce is not utilized for urethral reconstruction.<br/>\nMATERIALS AND METHODS<br/>\nPatient data of 452 patients managed between January 2010 and January 2023 were retrieved from the institutional registry. After excluding 39 patients lost to follow-up, 328 cases of primary hypospadias were evaluated. Preputioplasty was performed in 58 patients in whom the prepuce was not utilized for reconstruction. The feasibility of preputial reconstruction was assessed by placing a stay suture at the junction of the inner and outer preputial skin and evaluating its ability to be advanced over the glans. Reconstruction consisted of a longitudinal incision followed by two-layer approximation of the margins of the dorsal preputial hood.<br/>\nRESULTS<br/>\nPreputioplasty was performed in 58 (17.7%) patients, all belonging to the 0–5-year age group. Patients were followed for 12 months and evaluated for functional outcomes, cosmetic appearance, and postoperative complications. The majority demonstrated satisfactory urinary function and an aesthetically acceptable preputial appearance. Preputial dehiscence occurred in 6 patients, while 2 patients developed phimosis; these were managed with redo surgery or circumcision.<br/>\nCONCLUSION<br/>\nPreputioplasty in selected young patients undergoing hypospadias repair, when the prepuce is not required for urethral reconstruction, is a feasible technique with satisfactory functional and cosmetic outcomes and a low complication rate. Preservation and reconstruction of the prepuce may provide additional psychosocial and anatomical benefits while retaining a potential tissue reserve for future reconstruction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-41\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 41</p><h3>Hydrometrocolpos and Polydactyly in a Neonate: A Rare Presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Shubham Jain, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Mukesh Arya, Dr. Yogendra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Hydrometrocolpos and Polydactyly in a Neonate: A Rare Presentation<br/>\nINTRODUCTION<br/>\nNeonatal hydrometrocolpos (HMC) is a rare Müllerian duct anomaly resulting from obstruction of the vaginal outflow tract, leading to accumulation of mucus secretions. Although uncommon, it may present as a significant abdominopelvic mass with secondary urinary tract obstruction. Congenital HMC is described in association with syndromes such as McKusick–Kaufman and Bardet–Biedl syndromes.<br/>\nCASE PRESENTATION<br/>\nA female infant born at 35 weeks of gestation, with a birth weight of 2.360 kg, was noted to have postaxial polydactyly involving all four limbs and abdominal distension. Ultrasonography revealed a large fluid-filled lesion measuring 82 × 50 mm within the cervicovaginal canal, producing mass effect on the abdominal viscera. Bilateral hydronephrosis with a subcapsular urinoma was also noted.<br/>\nMANAGEMENT AND RESULTS<br/>\nThe infant underwent surgical intervention on day 19 of life. Intraoperatively, an imperforate hymen was identified. Hymenal incision was performed, resulting in drainage of thick, viscid secretions.<br/>\nCONCLUSION<br/>\nHydrometrocolpos is a rare neonatal condition that should be considered in the differential diagnosis of a midline abdominopelvic mass, particularly when associated with genital or limb anomalies. Early recognition and decompression of the accumulated secretions are essential to relieve mass effect and associated urinary tract obstruction. The coexistence of hydrometrocolpos, postaxial polydactyly and bilateral hydronephrosis represents an uncommon and clinically important presentation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-42\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 42</p><h3>Mini-Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy versus Retrograde Intrarenal Surgery for Moderate-Sized Renal Stones (10–20 mm) in Children: A Prospective Randomized Controlled Trial</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Arshad Khan , Dr Sudheer k Devana , Dr UK Mete , Dr Ravimohan S Mavuduru , Dr Girdhar S Bora , Dr Aditya P Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PGIMER CHANDIGARH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nThe optimal management of moderate-sized (10–20 mm) renal calculi in children remains controversial. Mini-percutaneous nephrolithotomy (Mini-PCNL) and retrograde intrarenal surgery (RIRS) are established minimally invasive options, but comparative randomized evidence in the pediatric population is limited.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods:<br/>\nA prospective randomized controlled trial was conducted between July 2024 and December 2025. Twenty-two children (≤14 years) with renal stones measuring 10–20 mm were randomized equally to Mini-PCNL (n=11) or RIRS (n=11). The primary outcome was stone-free rate (SFR) at one month. Secondary outcomes included operative parameters, postoperative complications (Clavien–Dindo classification), and hospital stay. Outcomes were analyzed using intention-to-treat (ITT), per-protocol (PP), and as-treated (AT) analyses.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nBaseline characteristics were comparable between groups except for a higher proportion of multiple stones in the RIRS arm. Two patients randomized to RIRS required conversion to Mini-PCNL because of failure of ureteric access despite pre-stenting. Mini-PCNL demonstrated significantly shorter lasing time (16.7±15.5 vs 40.3±29.1 minutes; p=0.031) and operative time (54.6±20.2 vs 77.3±22.8 minutes; p=0.023). ITT analysis showed a higher SFR with Mini-PCNL (100% vs 63.6%, p=0.09). On PP, Mini-PCNL achieved significantly higher SFR than RIRS (100% vs 55.6%; p=0.026). Postoperative complications and hospital stay were comparable between groups, with no increase in morbidity following Mini-PCNL.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nMini-PCNL provides superior single-session stone clearance and greater operative efficiency than RIRS for pediatric renal stones measuring 10–20 mm, while maintaining a comparable safety profile. In experienced hands, Mini-PCNL should be considered the preferred treatment option for appropriately selected children with moderate-sized renal calculi.<br/>\nKeywords: Pediatric urolithiasis, Mini-PCNL, RIRS, randomized controlled trial, renal stones, children.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-43\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 43</p><h3>Surgical management of Ileal conduit -vaginal fistula: A case report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr C Pamnani</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">FORTIS HOSPITAL MOHALI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: Ileal conduit -vaginal fistula is a rare, but challenging complication of urinary diversion. We report this case due to its rare occurrence and paucity of literature regarding its management.<br/>\nCase history: A 62-year-old woman with a history of carcinoma cervix, received chemo radiation therapy for cervical cancer in 2010 and developed radiation cystitis and enteritis. She presented with multiple episodes of life-threatening haematuria requiring multiple blood transfusions. Then she underwent robotic radical cystectomy and ileal conduit surgery in June 2025. Approximately, nine months after the surgery, she developed progressively worsening vaginal urine leakage, necessitating frequent diaper changes.<br/>\nDiagnostic workup, including MRI and CT Imaging, was performed to localized the fistulous tract. After multiple attempts of failed conservative management, surgical intervention in the form of laparotomy was under taken.<br/>\nExcision of the affected ileal conduit segment and small bowel resection and anastomosis was done.<br/>\nResult: Repair was successful and patient remains free from symptoms at 3 month follow up.<br/>\nConclusion : This case report highlights the rare complication of vaginal fistula in ileal conduit after radiation and its management. Timely diagnosis and intervention can reduce significant morbidity amongst patients.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-44\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 44</p><h3>SWEET DANGER: When Dapagliflozin Meets Pre-Existing Urological Pathologies (A Case Series of 5 patients)</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Deepak Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Fortis hospital mohali</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: Dapagliflozin improve glycaemic and cardio-renal outcomes but induce glucosuria, creating a favourable milieu for urinary tract colonisation. While mild genitourinary infections are well described, severe urosepsis remains under-reported. This series aims to describe the clinical profile, urological interventions, and outcomes of patients on dapagliflozin presenting with urosepsis.<br/>\nMethods: Five consecutive patients on dapagliflozin 10 mg once daily, admitted with urosepsis, were analysed retrospectively for demographic profile, comorbidities, imaging findings, microbiology, intervention required, and outcome.<br/>\nResults: Median age was 67 years.Median Dapagliflozin intake period was 10 months.Three patients were diabetic; the remaining two patients were on dapagliflozin for a cardiac indication, indicating that risk is not confined to diabetic recipients. Two patients had obstructive ureteric calculi on imaging &amp; one patient with obstructive renal calculus ,required emergency DJ stenting for obstructive uropathy with sepsis, while one had Grade 3 Prostatomegaly for which REZUM water vapour therapy done,one managed conservatively with intravenous antibiotics and supportive care alone. Three patients required intensive care admission, and two among them needed vasopressor support for septic shock. Urine cultures grew Escherichia coli in four patients and Citrobacter species in one; antibiotics were empirically escalated and subsequently tailored to sensitivity. Dapagliflozin was withheld in all patients at admission. All five recovered and were discharged following clinical and biochemical improvement.<br/>\nConclusion: Dapagliflozin-associated urinary infection can escalate rapidly to urosepsis, with a substantial proportion requiring urgent decompression and critical care support. The frequent coexistence of ureteric calculi suggests that obstruction and drug-induced glucosuria may act synergistically, and that stone-formers may represent a particularly vulnerable subgroup. Baseline urological assessment before initiating SGLT2 inhibitors, counselling regarding infective symptoms, and a low threshold for imaging and early drainage in febrile patients are advisable. Prospective evaluation is warranted to define the true incidence and risk stratification of this potentially serious adverse effect.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-45\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 45</p><h3>Outcomes of a Cost-Effective Pfannenstiel Incision for Graft Insertion in Robot-Assisted Kidney Transplantation: Does GelPoint™/Alexis™ Make a Difference?</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Kushwaha Swapnil Singh, Aggarwal Amit, Gaur Pankaj, Chauhan Upwan Kumar, Dassi Vimal, Kumar Anant</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPER SPECIALITY HOSPITAL, VAISHALI, GHAZIABAD</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIM:<br/>\nGraft insertion is a critical step in robot-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT), influencing rewarming ischemia time, operative workflow, procedural cost, and graft outcomes. GelPoint™/Alexis™ permits graft introduction without interrupting robotic docking but increases cost. A Pfannenstiel incision is a cost-effective alternative requiring temporary undocking and re-docking. We compared perioperative, functional, and outcomes of these techniques in a large single-centre RAKT series.</p><p>METHODS:<br/>\nA retrospective review of a prospectively maintained database included 428 consecutive RAKTs performed between 2016 and 2025 at a high-volume robotic transplant centre. Grafts were inserted using GelPoint™/Alexis™ in 81 patients and a Pfannenstiel incision in 347. Warm, cold, and rewarming ischemia times, graft insertion and docking time, renal function, delayed graft function (DGF), complications, and follow-up outcomes were compared.</p><p>RESULTS:<br/>\nWarm ischemia time (5.61 ± 1.34 vs. 5.78 ± 1.49 minutes; P=0.42) and cold ischemia time (46.8 ± 8.9 vs. 47.5 ± 9.2 minutes; P=0.67) were comparable. Graft insertion and docking time was significantly shorter with GelPoint™/Alexis™ (1.6 ± 0.8 vs. 9.4 ± 2.7 minutes; P&lt;0.001), resulting in lower rewarming ischemia time (48.6 ± 13.4 vs. 57.2 ± 15.6 minutes; P&lt;0.001). Nevertheless, serum creatinine on postoperative days 1, 5, and 7 was comparable. DGF occurred in 2.5% and 1.2% of patients, respectively (P=0.48). At mean follow-up of 29.2 ± 5.8 and 28.6 ± 6.1 months, serum creatinine remained comparable (1.21 ± 0.19 vs. 1.24 ± 0.22 mg/dL; P=0.52), without differences in graft survival or major complications.</p><p>CONCLUSION:<br/>\nPfannenstiel graft insertion is safe, reproducible, and cost-effective. Although it increases rewarming ischemia time, it does not adversely affect early or intermediate-term graft function, DGF, complications, or graft survival, supporting its routine use in resource-conscious RAKT programmes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-46\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 46</p><h3>Right-Sided Varicocele as a Rare Presentation of right spermatic cord Paraganglioma: A Case Report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR YAWAR NAZIR,DR ABDUL ROUF KHAWAJA,DR ARIF HAMID</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS J$K</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nIsolated right-sided varicocele is uncommon and warrants evaluation for an underlying retroperitoneal or renal pathology, particularly when large or associated with an abdominal mass. Paragangliomas are rare extra-adrenal neuroendocrine tumors that can occur at unusual retroperitoneal locations. We report an unusual case of a cord paraganglioma presenting as an isolated right-sided varicocele.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 34-year-old man presented with a 2-year history of right hemiscrotal swelling and heaviness. Examination revealed a large isolated right grade 3 varicocele with a palpable right iliac fossa mass. Ultrasonography demonstrated a large predominantly solid retroperitoneal lesion causing compression of the right gonadal vessels. Contrast-enhanced CT revealed an 80 × 87 mm avidly enhancing right retroperitoneal mass situated below the bifurcation of the right common iliac vessels. Biochemical evaluation showed normal 24-hour urinary metanephrine, normetanephrine and VMA levels, suggesting a non-functional tumor. Other retroperitoneal tumor markers were also within normal limits.<br/>\nResults: The patient underwent surgical excision. Intraoperatively, a highly vascular tumor was found arising from the right spermatic cord, necessitating right orchidectomy due to continuity of the gonadal pedicle with the tumor. Histopathological examination confirmed paraganglioma, with immunohistochemistry positive for synaptophysin, chromogranin, GATA-3 and S-100. The postoperative course was uneventful.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nAlthough right-sided varicocele is often attributed to renal or retroperitoneal pathology, rare vascular tumors such as paraganglioma should also be considered. A new, large, isolated or non-reducible right-sided varicocele should prompt appropriate abdominal imaging to exclude an underlying retroperitoneal mass. Awareness of this rare presentation can facilitate early diagnosis and appropriate surgical management</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-47\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 47</p><h3>UNUSUAL BLADDER MASSES PRESENTING WITH LUTS-DIFFERENT SITUATIONS, UNIQUE MANAGEMENT STRATERGIES</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Deepak, Dr Aravind, Dr Anup Kumar, Dr Pawan Vasudeva, Dr Siddharth Yadav, Dr Ruchi Mittal, Dr Niraj Kumar, Dr Sandeep Kumar, Dr Rohan Nautiyal, Dr Abhishek Johari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC AND SJH, DELHI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nA bladder or perivesical mass presenting with lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) is presumed urothelial carcinoma, and the uncommon cystic, inflammatory, mesenchymal and infective causes are recognised only once radical surgery is planned. We present four such masses in which the situation differed at every step — presentation, diagnostic route and definitive treatment — and each demanded its own bladder-preserving strategy.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nFour patients with LUTS and a bladder or perivesical mass at a single tertiary unit were reviewed. Symptoms, imaging, cystoscopy, biopsy and definitive histopathology were recorded. Outcomes were concordance of the pre-treatment label with final histology, bladder preservation and symptom resolution at minimum three months.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nTwo masses were reported as malignant before tissue diagnosis; none proved malignant. A 32-year-old man with 2-year voiding LUTS (IPSS 18, Qmax 3.24 mL/s, PVR 270 mL) had a 16 × 15 mm multiloculated ball-valve bladder neck cyst; transurethral deroofing yielded Brunn's nests and Qmax rose to 19 mL/s. A 62-year-old woman with voiding LUTS and haematuria had a 3 × 3 cm lateral wall mass worked up as cancer; histology showed transmural eosinophilic cystitis, and antihistamine with tapering prednisolone normalised cystoscopy. A 37-year-old woman with a 45 mm submucosal leiomyoma at the anterior wall and bladder neck underwent robot-assisted bladder-sparing excision with bladder neck reconstruction — margin free, no leak. A 33-year-old woman with storage LUTS and a urachal mass read as adenocarcinoma was unresectable at laparoscopy; biopsy showed Actinomyces, and six months of penicillin-based therapy achieved complete radiological resolution, disease-free at one year.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nFour masses demanded four strategies: endoscopic deroofing, medical therapy, robotic bladder-sparing excision and reconstruction, and antimicrobials alone. Cystoscopy and histology, not imaging, established every diagnosis, and a radiological label of malignancy proved no mandate to resect. All four bladders were preserved with complete symptom resolution.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-48\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 48</p><h3>ENIGMATIC HEMATURIA UNMASKED: RETROPERITONEAL LYMPHANGIECTASIA WITH CHYLURIA PRESENTING AS RECURRENT HEMATURIA — A CASE REPORT</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shivam Upadhayaya</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS RISHIKESH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Recurrent hematuria refractory to conventional urological workup poses a significant diagnostic challenge. Retroperitoneal lymphangiectasia — a rare, non-neoplastic cystic dilation of retroperitoneal lymphatic channels — is an uncommonly recognised cause, particularly when chyluria is subtle or absent. We report a case of bilateral retroperitoneal lymphangiectasia presenting as enigmatic chyle-hematuria, successfully managed with retrograde renal pelvic instillation sclerotherapy.<br/>\nCase Report: A 26-year-old male presented with 8 months of recurrent painless chyle-hematuria with intermittent left flank pain. He had previously undergone renal mass biopsy at an outside institution, which was histopathologically normal. Urine AFB (UFMC) was negative. Urine triglycerides were elevated at 7.75 mg/dL, confirming chylous urine. CT urography demonstrated a non-enhancing tubular cystic structure in the aortocaval and para-aortic regions (craniocaudal length 11.3 cm), consistent with retroperitoneal lymphangiectasia. Cystoscopy with bilateral retrograde pyelography confirmed bilateral pyelolymphatic reflux, more pronounced on the left. The patient underwent retrograde renal pelvic instillation sclerotherapy with 5 mL Urografin followed by 9 doses of 0.2% Povidone-Iodine at 8-hourly intervals. The post-operative period was uneventful, chyluria resolved completely, and the patient was discharged on post-operative day 4.<br/>\nDiscussion: Pyelolymphatic reflux on retrograde pyelography is pathognomonic of pyelolymphatic fistula. Non-filarial retroperitoneal lymphangiectasia remains underdiagnosed due to its rarity and variable presentation. A non-enhancing para-aortic cystic lesion on CT in a young patient with chyle-hematuria should prompt retrograde pyelography. Retrograde renal pelvic Povidone-Iodine sclerotherapy offers a minimally invasive, endoscopic solution with reported success rates of 75–92%, obviating the need for open surgery.<br/>\nConclusion: Retroperitoneal lymphangiectasia should be considered in the differential diagnosis of enigmatic hematuria. CT urography combined with retrograde pyelography establishes the diagnosis, and retrograde sclerotherapy is a safe and effective treatment. This case highlights the importance of a lymphatic aetiology in unexplained hematuria and adds to the sparse global literature on this rare entity.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-49\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 49</p><h3>Beyond the Kidney: En-Bloc Multi-Visceral Resection for a Pancreatic Tumor Masquerading as RCC</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Varun Gowda, Dr Suresh Goyal, Dr Abhishek Chabbra, Dr Abhay Gaur, Dr Kawaljit Kaura</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS Bathinda</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Abstract:<br/>\nLarge retroperitoneal mass which invades into adjacent organs often mimic locally advanced primary renal malignancy. A very unusual case of a suspected locally advanced renal cell carcinoma (RCC) requiring extensive multi-visceral resection with an unexpected histopathological diagnosis.<br/>\nThe patient was a 40-year-old gentleman, who presented with left flank pain, constipation, and hematochezia. Sonography showed a large left renal mass. On evaluation with CECT Abdomen pelvis the patient had large mass extending from the upper and middle poles of the left kidney and showing direct local invasion of the tail of the pancreas, splenic flexure of the colon and the left adrenal gland.</p><p>Management: After a multidisciplinary discussion with Surgical Gastroenterology, and thorough patient optimization, a combined surgical procedure was performed. The en-bloc left radical nephrectomy, adrenalectomy, distal pancreatectomy, splenectomy and Descending colon with a primary colo-colic anastomosis were performed. Although the postoperative period was challenging, the patient was discharged on postoperative day 20.</p><p>Results: Final histopathological analysis was an unexpected diagnosis. The tumor was determined to be a well-differentiated pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor (pNET) and not the presumed primary RCC, with contiguous tumor invasion directly into the kidney and adjacent viscera.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-50\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 50</p><h3>Urachal sinus due to migration of fish bone from bowel mimicking Urachal Malignancy: A Rare Diagnostic Challenge</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Omar Mukhtar , Prof Dr Arif Hamid Bhat , Dr Abdul Rauf Khwaja</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS . Sgr , J&amp;K</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims : Migration of ingested foreign bodies into the urinary tract is exceedingly uncommon. Fish bone migration into a patent urachus presenting as a urachal mass with discharging sinus, an exceptionally rare entity and may closely mimic urachal carcinoma, posing a significant diagnostic dilemma.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods: A 39-year-old male presented with lower urinary tract symptoms, lower abdominal pain, discharging umblical sinus and a progressively enlarging infraumbilical swelling of one month's duration. Ultrasonography demonstrated a 7 × 8 cm mass extending from the bladder dome to the umbilicus. Contrast-enhanced CT revealed an irregular enhancing lesion with central necrosis and a linear calcified focus, raising strong suspicion for urachal malignancy. Cystoscopy demonstrated a broad-based lesion at the bladder dome.<br/>\nResults : The patient underwent exploration, had dense adhesions between mass and bowel loops. Followed by en bloc excision of the urachal mass with partial cystectomy. Gross examination revealed a 2.5-cm fish bone within the lesion. Histopathology confirmed foreign body granulomatous inflammation without evidence of malignancy. Postoperative recovery was uneventful.<br/>\nConclusion: Fish bone migration into the urachus is an extremely rare cause of an inflammatory mass that can closely resemble urachal carcinoma on clinical and radiological evaluation. Although contrast-enhanced CT is the imaging modality of choice, definitive diagnosis may only be established following surgical excision and histopathological examination.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-51\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 51</p><h3>Androgen-Secreting Pediatric Adrenocortical Carcinoma Presenting as Precocious Puberty in a Toddler</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Rajat Gupta, Dr. Nihal Singh, Dr. Somendra Bansal, Dr. Shivam Priyadarshi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS HOSPITAL, JAIPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nPediatric adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is an exceptionally rare malignancy, and hormonally functional tumors may present with endocrine manifestations before local or systemic symptoms become apparent. Virilization and gonadotropin-independent precocious puberty should raise suspicion for an androgen-secreting adrenal neoplasm.</p><p>Case Presentation:<br/>\nA 1-year-8-month-old boy presented with progressive penile enlargement and development of pubic hair. Clinical examination revealed marked virilization with Tanner stage III pubic hair. Hormonal evaluation demonstrated markedly elevated testosterone (649 ng/mL) and dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (946 µg/dL), with suppressed luteinizing hormone and follicle-stimulating hormone, consistent with peripheral precocious puberty. Contrast-enhanced CT abdomen revealed a well-circumscribed heterogeneous left suprarenal mass measuring 2.5 × 1.5 cm. The child underwent open left adrenalectomy with regional lymph-node excision. Histopathology demonstrated diffuse architecture, marked pleomorphism, increased mitotic activity (&gt;20/50 HPF), focal necrosis, dystrophic calcification and lymphovascular invasion. Surgical margins were negative and the excised lymph node showed reactive hyperplasia without metastasis. Immunohistochemistry was positive for synaptophysin, Melan-A and inhibin, confirming adrenocortical differentiation. The pathological stage was pT1N0Mx. At 42-day follow-up, serum testosterone had normalized and DHEA-S had significantly declined.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nAndrogen-secreting pediatric ACC can present solely as rapidly progressive virilization and precocious puberty, even with a small and apparently localized adrenal lesion. Early recognition of endocrine manifestations, appropriate hormonal evaluation and cross-sectional imaging are crucial for diagnosis. Complete surgical excision remains the cornerstone of treatment. Despite an early anatomical stage, adverse histopathological features such as high mitotic activity, necrosis and lymphovascular invasion may indicate aggressive biological behavior and warrant meticulous long-term surveillance.</p><p>Keywords: Pediatric adrenocortical carcinoma; androgen-secreting tumor; precocious puberty; virilization; adrenal tumor; adrenalectomy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-52\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 52</p><h3>Recurrent Graft Ureteric Stricture Following Renal Transplantation Managed by Native Ureteropyelostomy: A Salvage Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Bhagwati Chandra Verma,Dr Anuj Kumar Yadav,Dr Praveen Kumar Yadav,Dr Deepak Rathi,Dr Prasun Ghosh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta The Medicity</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nUreteric complications occur in 2–10% of renal transplant recipients, with distal ureteric stricture being the most common. Ischemia related to compromised periureteric blood supply is a major contributing factor. While initial management includes ureteric stenting, percutaneous nephrostomy (PCN), endourological intervention, and ureteric reimplantation, recurrent strictures after reconstruction remain challenging. We present a case of recurrent graft ureteric stricture successfully managed by native ureteropyelostomy.<br/>\nCase Presentation:<br/>\nA 66-year-old male with end-stage renal disease underwent live donor renal transplantation in 2021 using a left donor kidney with single renal artery, vein, and ureter. Ureteroneocystostomy was performed using the Lich-Gregoir technique over a 6/16 DJ stent. Following stent removal, serum creatinine increased from 0.9 to 5.8 mg/dL, with graft hydroureteronephrosis. PCN decompression resulted in improvement of renal function, followed by DJ stenting. Due to recurrent obstruction after stent removal, surgical ureteric reimplantation performed.<br/>\nThree years later, the patient presented with recurrent graft dysfunction and hydronephrosis. Repeat PCN improved renal parameters. Ultrasound demonstrated moderate graft hydronephrosis, while nephrostography showed a stricturous distal graft ureter. In view of recurrent distal ureteric stricture following previous reconstruction, native ureteropyelostomy was planned. Retrograde pyelography of the native ureter was performed, followed by mobilization and transection of the native ureter. The ureter was anastomosed to the graft renal pelvis over a 6/16 DJ stent. The existing PCN facilitated intraoperative identification of the graft pelvis.<br/>\nOutcome and Conclusion:<br/>\nPostoperatively, renal function improved and adequate drainage was confirmed. PCN was removed on postoperative day 21 following nephrostography, followed by DJ stent removal. The patient remained asymptomatic with stable graft function on follow-up.<br/>\nNative ureteropyelostomy is an effective salvage option for recurrent or complex graft ureteric strictures. It bypasses the diseased ureter, utilizes a well-vascularized native ureter, avoids repeat bladder reconstruction. PCN-assisted intraoperative localization can facilitate safe reconstruction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-53\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 53</p><h3>Nocardiosis Of the Urinary tract - A Rare Infection</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Rajat Upadhyay, Dr Pankaj Wadhwa, Dr Rajesh Ahlawat, Dr Harkirat Singh Talwar, Dr Praanjal Gupta</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super Speciality Hospital Noida</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIM<br/>\nDisseminated nocardiosis involving the genitourinary tract, particularly the prostate, is rare and poses urological challenges. We report disseminated nocardiosis presenting with renal and multifocal prostatic abscesses and cerebral lesions, highlighting diagnostic clues and transurethral deroofing for source control.</p><p>METHODS<br/>\nA 70-year-old man with diabetes, hypertension, chronic kidney disease and coronary artery disease presented with fever, lower urinary tract symptoms and respiratory distress. He was septic, with suprapubic tenderness and an enlarged, tender prostate. Investigations showed leukocytosis, anaemia, hyponatraemia, pyuria and microscopic haematuria; chest imaging demonstrated bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. Blood cultures revealed irregular Gram-positive branching filamentous bacilli that were weakly acid-fast, consistent with nocardiosis. Imaging showed a right renal subcapsular collection and multiple prostatic abscesses; brain magnetic resonance imaging revealed small parietal infective lesions.</p><p>RESULTS<br/>\nDue to persistent sepsis with multifocal prostatic abscesses, the patient underwent cystoscopy, optical internal urethrotomy, and transurethral resection with deroofing of the abscesses, achieving decompression and tissue diagnosis. The renal collection was managed conservatively. Prolonged nocardiosis-directed combination antimicrobial therapy was administered with nephrology input because of chronic kidney disease and sulfonamide-related metabolic issues, resulting in clinical and radiological improvement.</p><p>CONCLUSIONS<br/>\nThis case illustrates disseminated nocardiosis with simultaneous pulmonary, renal, prostatic and cerebral involvement. Early recognition is essential when weakly acid-fast branching bacilli are detected in blood or deep samples. Multidisciplinary management, timely drainage of larger or multiloculated prostatic abscesses, and individualized prolonged antimicrobial therapy are central to successful outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-54\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 54</p><h3>Algorithm-Based Reconstruction of Vesicovaginal Fistula: A Decade of Tailored Surgical Management and the Feasibility of Robotic Reconstruction</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Farhat Saleem, Dr. Anshuman Agarwal, Dr. Vishal Kashyp, Dr. Rajan Prasad Gupta, Dr. Rizul Prashar, Dr. Saifullah Nizam.</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">indraprastha Apollo Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo evaluate the clinical profile, surgical management, and outcomes of vesicovaginal fistula (VVF) repair at our centre, describe the evolution from conventional to robotic reconstruction, assess the feasibility of the robotic approach, and propose a practical management algorithm based on fistula complexity.<br/>\nMaterials and methods:<br/>\nA retrospective review was performed of 11 consecutive patients undergoing VVF repair at our centre. Patient demographics, etiology, fistula characteristics, previous repair status, operative approach, perioperative outcomes and follow-up were analysed. Surgical strategy was individualized according to fistula location, complexity and prior interventions. An institutional management algorithm was formulated based on this experience.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nThe median age was 48 years . VVF followed benign hysterectomy in 9 patients, radical hysterectomy in 1 and obstetric injury in 1. Four patients had recurrent fistulas after failed repair elsewhere. Three patients underwent transvaginal repair with Martius flap, six underwent open transabdominal O'Conor repair with omental interposition, and two underwent robot-assisted transabdominal repair for complex supratrigonal fistulas. Robotic reconstruction was successfully completed in all selected patients without conversion or major perioperative complications, demonstrating its technical feasibility in experienced hands. Median hospital stay was 5 days. Per-urethral catheters were removed after 1 week and suprapubic catheters after 2 weeks. No patient required reoperation. At a median follow-up of 18 months, all patients achieved successful fistula closure. with complete continence and no recurrence,<br/>\ncorresponding to a 100% success rate<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nAn algorithm-based approach enables tailored selection of vaginal, open, or robotic VVF repair according to fistula complexity. Vaginal repair remains appropriate for simple low fistulas, whereas transabdominal reconstruction is preferred for recurrent and supratrigonal defects. In the hands of experienced surgeons, robotic extravesical VVF repair is a safe and effective technique with outstanding cure rates comparable to those of the conventional approachs and hence expanding the armamentarium of reconstructive urology.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-55\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 55</p><h3>Acute Abdomen from a Giant Haemorrhagic Adrenal Cyst: Successful Laparoscopic Adrenalectomy in a Rare Surgical Emergency</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Farhat Saleem, Dr. Anshuman Agarwal, Dr. Saifullah Nizam, Dr. Rajan Prasad Gupta, Dr. Rizul Prashar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">indraprastha apollo hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo highlight the rare presentation of a giant haemorrhagic adrenal cyst as an acute abdomen and demonstrate the feasibility of laparoscopic adrenalectomy in the emergency management of symptomatic adrenal cysts.<br/>\nMaterials and methods:<br/>\nA 25-year-old woman presented with a two-week history of upper abdominal pain that worsened suddenly, with severe right-sided chest, upper abdominal, and back pain. Contrast_x0002_enhanced CT revealed a large haemorrhagic right adrenal cyst with surrounding inflammatory fat stranding, inferior displacement of the right kidney, and indentation of the liver. The right adrenal gland was not separately visualized. Biochemical evaluation showed no evidence of hormonal hypersecretion. Owing to persistent severe symptoms and imaging suggestive of intracystic haemorrhage, the patient underwent laparoscopic right adrenalectomy with complete excision of the adrenal cyst.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nIntraoperatively, a large haemorrhagic cyst arising from the lateral limb of right adrenal gland was identified, extending from the inferior surface of the liver to the lower pole of the kidney.The cyst was excised completely along with the involved adrenal tissue using a laparoscopic approach without any intraoperative complications or conversion. The postoperative course was uneventful, with gradual recovery, drain removal and discharge in stable condition. Histopathology confirmed a benign cyst.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nAlthough adrenal cysts have been described previously, they remain uncommon lesions.  Most of these lesions are found incidentally; although they can cause symptoms due to tumour expansion or, in rare cases, hyperfunctioning endocrine lesions or shock from blood loss into the cyst. Haemorrhage into an adrenal cyst is a rare but important cause of acute abdomen requiring a high index of suspicion. Following exclusion of hormonal hyperfunction, timely surgical intervention is warranted for symptomatic or complicated lesions. This case demonstrates that laparoscopic adrenalectomy is a safe, feasible, and effective treatment even for giant haemorrhagic adrenal cysts, providing excellent perioperative outcomes in experienced hands.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-56\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 56</p><h3>A Solitary Kidney with Multiple Stones and Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer: A Challenging Stone-Guided PCNL Followed by Radical Cystectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">MANJEET KUMAR, ARJUN SHARMA, PAWAN KAUNDAL, GIRISH SHARMA, PAMPOSH RAINA, KAILASH CHANDER BARWAL</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIMSS SHIMLA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims</p><p>To present the challenging management of a patient with muscle-invasive bladder cancer (MIBC), persistent hematuria, a solitary functioning kidney, and multiple renal calculi, where retrograde access was not feasible and stone clearance required a tailored percutaneous approach.</p><p>Methods</p><p>A patient with a solitary functioning kidney presented with persistent hematuria and was diagnosed with muscle-invasive bladder cancer. Imaging revealed multiple renal calculi with a significant stone burden in the solitary kidney.</p><p>The major challenge was obtaining access to the collecting system, as retrograde access from below was not possible. A stone-guided PCNL approach was therefore adopted. Multiple percutaneous punctures were strategically performed, targeting individual stone-bearing calyces, to achieve adequate access and facilitate complete stone clearance while minimizing injury to the solitary renal unit.</p><p>Following successful clearance of the renal calculi and recovery, the patient underwent open radical cystectomy for definitive management of MIBC.</p><p>Results</p><p>Stone-guided multiple percutaneous punctures enabled access to the different stone-bearing calyces despite the absence of retrograde access. PCNL resulted in successful clearance of the multiple renal calculi with preservation of the solitary kidney.</p><p>The patient subsequently underwent open radical cystectomy and had an uneventful postoperative recovery. Renal function was preserved, and the patient recovered well following definitive oncological treatment.</p><p>Conclusions</p><p>The combination of MIBC, a solitary functioning kidney, multiple renal calculi, and inaccessible retrograde access presents a unique surgical challenge. Stone-guided multiple percutaneous punctures can provide effective and precise access for complete stone clearance in such complex anatomy. A staged approach with PCNL followed by open radical cystectomy enabled both preservation of renal function and definitive treatment of the bladder malignancy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-57\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 57</p><h3>A Solitary Kidney with Multiple Stones and Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer: A Challenging Stone-Guided PCNL Followed by Radical Cystectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">MANJEET KUMAR, ARJUN SHARMA, PAWAN KAUNDAL, GIRISH KUMAR SHARMA , PAMPOSH RAINA, KAILASH CHANDER BARWAL</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIMSS SHIMLA HP INDIA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo present the challenging management of a patient with muscle-invasive bladder cancer (MIBC), persistent hematuria, a solitary functioning kidney, and multiple renal calculi, where retrograde access was not feasible and stone clearance required a tailored percutaneous approach.</p><p>Methods</p><p>A patient with a solitary functioning kidney presented with persistent hematuria and was diagnosed with muscle-invasive bladder cancer. Imaging revealed multiple renal calculi with a significant stone burden in the solitary kidney.</p><p>The major challenge was obtaining access to the collecting system, as retrograde access from below was not possible. A stone-guided PCNL approach was therefore adopted. Multiple percutaneous punctures were strategically performed, targeting individual stone-bearing calyces, to achieve adequate access and facilitate complete stone clearance while minimizing injury to the solitary renal unit.</p><p>Following successful clearance of the renal calculi and recovery, the patient underwent open radical cystectomy for definitive management of MIBC.</p><p>Results</p><p>Stone-guided multiple percutaneous punctures enabled access to the different stone-bearing calyces despite the absence of retrograde access. PCNL resulted in successful clearance of the multiple renal calculi with preservation of the solitary kidney.</p><p>The patient subsequently underwent open radical cystectomy and had an uneventful postoperative recovery. Renal function was preserved, and the patient recovered well following definitive oncological treatment.</p><p>Conclusions</p><p>The combination of MIBC, a solitary functioning kidney, multiple renal calculi, and inaccessible retrograde access presents a unique surgical challenge. Stone-guided multiple percutaneous punctures can provide effective and precise access for complete stone clearance in such complex anatomy. A staged approach with PCNL followed by open radical cystectomy enabled both preservation of renal function and definitive treatment of the bladder malignancy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-58\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 58</p><h3>En Bloc Kidney Transplantation From a 5-Year-Old Deceased Donor Into an Adult Recipient in an Emerging Deceased Donor Program : Immediate Graft Function, Conservative Leak Salvage, and Quantitative Follow-up</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Ujjwal Das, Dr. A S Sandhu,, Dr. Gautam Ram Choudhary, Dr. Mahendra Singh, Dr. Shiv Charan Navriya, Dr. Deepak Prakash Bhirud</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims. To report en bloc kidney transplantation (EBKT) from a 5-year-old deceased brain-dead donor into an adult recipient, and to describe the surgical technique, the non-operative management of an early urinary leak, and objective functional recovery — illustrating the feasibility of paediatric EBKT as a strategy to expand the deceased-donor pool.<br/>\nMethods. Single-patient case report. A 33-year-old man with end-stage renal disease of undetermined aetiology, on maintenance haemodialysis for three years, received an ABO-compatible EBKT from a 5-year-old deceased brain-dead donor. Both kidneys were transplanted en bloc into the right iliac fossa using the donor aorta and inferior vena cava, with two separate double-J–stented ureteroneocystostomies. A zero-hour donor biopsy was performed for donor proteinuria. Induction was rabbit antithymocyte globulin, with tacrolimus, mycophenolate and prednisolone maintenance; valproate was switched to levetiracetam to protect calcineurin-inhibitor levels. Outcome measures were serum creatinine, graft perfusion and complications, and split renal function and effective renal plasma flow (ERPF) on ⁹⁹ᵐTc-EC renography. Warm and cold ischaemia times were 15 minutes and 4 hours 40 minutes.<br/>\nResults. The zero-hour biopsy showed no acute or chronic injury (Banff all-zero). The graft functioned immediately with no delayed graft function; serum creatinine reached a nadir of 1.02 mg/dL by postoperative day (POD) 26. An early urinary leak (POD-9; drain-fluid creatinine 13.16 mg/dL, approximately 13 times serum), localised to the lower-pole ureteroneocystostomy, was managed conservatively with catheter drainage and resolved without reoperation. At approximately 5.5 months, serum creatinine was 1.19 mg/dL and ⁹⁹ᵐTc-EC renography showed balanced split function (upper 45% / lower 55%) with a total ERPF of 150.5 mL/min.<br/>\nConclusions. En-bloc paediatric kidney transplantation into an adult gave immediate function with balanced split function, no DGF, and successful conservative management of an early urinary leak — a practical way to expand the deceased-donor pool.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-59\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 59</p><h3>Urothelial carcinoma arising in the upper moiety of a left duplex collecting system: A rare case report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Mohit Sethi, Dr Shivam Priyadarshi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS MEDICAL COLLEGE AND HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims</p><p>To highlight the diagnostic and surgical challenges of upper tract urothelial carcinoma (UTUC) arising within the upper moiety of a congenital duplex collecting system.</p><p>Methods</p><p>A 76-year-old chronic smoker presented with painless intermittent gross hematuria with serpentine clots for five months. Ultrasonography revealed a duplex left pelvicalyceal system with a dilated upper moiety and a 5.6 × 2.3 cm fungating mass involving the upper ureter. Contrast-enhanced CT with angiography demonstrated a duplex left collecting system with two ureters joining at the S1 level, marked dilatation and cortical thinning of the superior moiety, and an irregular enhancing lesion involving the superior-moiety calyces and ureter. Urine cytology was suspicious for high-grade urothelial malignancy. Cystoscopy identified two left ureteric orifices, while selective retrograde pyelography delineated both moieties.</p><p>Results</p><p>Staging evaluation showed no distant metastatic disease. The patient underwent complete left nephroureterectomy with bladder-cuff excision. Histopathology revealed high-grade invasive papillary urothelial carcinoma with squamous differentiation involving the superior moiety. The tumor extended through the muscularis propria into adjacent renal parenchyma/pelvicalyceal region, with lymphovascular invasion. Surgical margins and perinephric fat were free of tumor, and the examined regional lymph node was negative. The final pathological stage was pT3 Nx.</p><p>Conclusions</p><p>UTUC arising within a duplex collecting system is a rare entity presenting unique diagnostic and surgical challenges. Accurate delineation of the duplicated anatomy and moiety-specific tumor involvement using cross-sectional imaging, cystoscopy and selective retrograde pyelography is essential for diagnosis and surgical planning. Radical nephroureterectomy with bladder-cuff excision remains an appropriate treatment for high-grade, high-volume UTUC in this setting.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-60\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 60</p><h3>Unveiling the Ureter: Laparoscopic Ureterolysis with Intra-peritonealization for Idiopathic Retroperitoneal Fibrosis—A minimally Invasive Surgical Series</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Rahul Anand</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS , JODHPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo describe the surgical technique and short-term outcomes of laparoscopic bilateral ureterolysis<br/>\nwith intraperitonealization in patients with idiopathic retroperitoneal fibrosis (RPF) who remained<br/>\nstent-dependent or refractory to medical therapy.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nThis retrospective case series included four patients with imaging/biopsy-confirmed idiopathic RPF<br/>\ncausing bilateral ureteric obstruction: three male aged 31, 39, and 60 years and one woman aged 56<br/>\nyears. All initially underwent bilateral DJ stenting and corticosteroid therapy for 13, 32, 17, and 24<br/>\nmonths, respectively, but remained stent-dependent. Laparoscopic bilateral ureterolysis was<br/>\nperformed using a standardized side-to-side modified lateral decubitus approach. The technique<br/>\nincluded colonic mobilization, broad Toldt-line peritoneal flap creation, gonadal-vein-guided ureteric<br/>\nidentification, systematic distal-to-proximal ureterolysis, and intraperitonealization using<br/>\nmoon-shaped peritoneal-flap fixation to the lateral abdominal wall. Perioperative complications,<br/>\nrenal functional recovery, and successful DJ stent removal were assessed.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nAll four procedures were completed laparoscopically without conversion or intraoperative<br/>\ncomplications, with uneventful postoperative recovery. Patient 1 demonstrated non-obstructive<br/>\nbilateral drainage on diuretic renography (split function 65%/35%), creatinine normalization to 0.9<br/>\nmg/dL, and remained stent-free at 1-year follow-up. Patient 2, with severe renal impairment and<br/>\nelevated IgG4, showed improvement in creatinine from 7.9 to 0.9 mg/dL. Patient 3 demonstrated<br/>\nmarked renal recovery, with creatinine improving from 7.61 to 1.1 mg/dL. Patient 4, a 56 year female<br/>\nwith elevated IgG4 and severe renal impairment after 24 months of steroid therapy, showed<br/>\nimprovement in creatinine from 8.35 to 2.03 mg/dL. All patients successfully underwent DJ stent<br/>\nremoval with preserved renal function.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nLaparoscopic bilateral ureterolysis with intraperitonealization using systematic gonadal-vein-guided<br/>\ndissection and moon-shaped peritoneal-flap fixation is a safe, reproducible, and effective minimally<br/>\ninvasive approach for RPF-related ureteric obstruction refractory to medical therapy. It provides<br/>\nureteral decompression while avoiding open surgical morbidity and obviating the need for omental<br/>\nwrapping.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-61\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 61</p><h3>Childhood trauma to adult presentation: A post-traumatic corporourethral vascular fistula with penile curvature managed by single-stage surgical repair</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Digvijay Singh Tanwar, Dr Shiv Charan Navriya, Dr Chintapali Ravi Chandra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To report an unusual case of corporourethral vascular malformation causing childhood-onset painless perurethral bleeding and penile curvature, and to describe its diagnostic work-up and surgical management.</p><p>Methods: A 27-year-old male presented with recurrent painless perurethral bleeding since childhood, predominantly during erections, following childhood blunt penile trauma, along with progressive penile curvature on erection. Examination revealed a non-tender, non-pulsatile 1.5 cm nodule at the left penile base and curvature exceeding 70 degrees. Colour Doppler suggested a vascular malformation with an adjacent scar-like hypoechoic area, and MRI showed a small enhancing vascular tangle within bilateral corpora cavernosa. Digital subtraction angiography excluded a true arteriovenous malformation, and urethroscopy showed no discrete mass. The patient underwent cystopanendoscopy and penile exploration after degloving, with intraoperative assessment and corrective surgery.</p><p>Results: Cystopanendoscopy showed mucosal lacerations with suspected vascular channels in the proximal urethra; the remaining urethra, prostate, and bladder were normal. On exploration, a corporourethral vascular connection near the left penile base was ligated and divided. A 1.5 x 1 cm supratunical aneurysm over the left tunica albuginea, with feeding vascular pedicles, was excised. Intraoperative erection testing confirmed residual left curvature of under 20 degrees, corrected with an eight-dot plication of the right tunica albuginea, with complete correction on repeat testing. Operative time was 2.5 hours with 30 mL blood loss. Recovery was uneventful, with a healthy wound (Southampton grade 0) and satisfactory voiding after catheter removal on postoperative day 2.</p><p>Conclusions: Corporourethral vascular communication is a rare, under-recognised cause of recurrent perurethral bleeding and penile curvature, often following childhood trauma. Combined Doppler, MRI, angiography, and urethroscopy help characterise the lesion even when angiography is non-diagnostic. Surgical exploration with ligation of the abnormal connection, aneurysm excision, and simultaneous curvature correction offers a safe, single-stage, definitive treatment.</p><p>Conflict of Interest: No conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-62\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 62</p><h3>CANDIDA GLABRATA FUNGAL BALL OF THE URINARY BLADDER FOLLOWING CANAGLIFLOZIN THERAPY REQUIRING CYSTECTOMY</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Mohit kaushik,</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta-The medicity gurugram</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>CANDIDA GLABRATA FUNGAL BALL OF THE URINARY BLADDER FOLLOWING CANAGLIFLOZIN THERAPY REQUIRING CYSTECTOMY<br/>\n1. AIMS<br/>\nTo highlight a rare, severe Candida glabrata urinary tract infection associated with canagliflozin, causing recurrent invasive bladder fungal balls and refractory fungal cystitis requiring salvage cystectomy.<br/>\n2. METHODS<br/>\nWe present the clinical course of a 71-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus who developed progressive lower urinary tract symptoms after starting canagliflozin. Evaluation included urine cultures, ultrasonography, cystoscopy, endoscopic resection and histopathology. Endoscopic clearance and prolonged intravesical antifungal therapy were undertaken. Responses were assessed, with multidisciplinary evaluation for surgery.<br/>\n3. RESULTS<br/>\nUrine culture grew Candida glabrata, and cystoscopy revealed an adherent fungal bezoar involving the bladder wall. Complete endoscopic removal was impossible because of dense adherence and suspected detrusor involvement. Histopathology confirmed fungal elements without malignancy. Despite repeated resections and treatment with fluconazole, 5-flucytosine, systemic amphotericin B and 16 days of intravesical amphotericin B irrigation, symptoms and candiduria persisted. Amphotericin B was complicated by acute kidney injury and discontinued. Recurrent fungal bezoars, persistent invasive bladder involvement and failure of medical and endoscopic treatment led to salvage radical cystectomy with ileal conduit diversion. Histopathology showed severe chronic fungal cystitis with diffuse wall involvement. Postoperatively, symptoms resolved and candiduria was eradicated.</p><p>4. CONCLUSIONS<br/>\nCandida glabrata fungal balls are rare but can cause invasive, antifungal-refractory cystitis. SGLT2 inhibitor-associated glucosuria may promote fungal infection. While antifungal therapy and endoscopic evacuation remain initial treatments, refractory invasive disease may require surgery. Early prompt recognition, repeated microbiological and endoscopic assessment, and multidisciplinary management are essential.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-63\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 63</p><h3>Persistent nephrocutaneous sinus due to extruded renal calculus in a pediatric patient with treated genito-urinary tuberculosis</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Y S Chundawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Santokba Durlabji Memorial Hospital,Jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:</p><p>Genitourinary tuberculosis may lead to nephrocutaneous sinus formation in some cases. However, persistent non-healing sinus after adequate anti-tubercular therapy (ATT) is rare, particularly in pediatric patients. We present a rare case of persistent nephrocutaneous sinus following completed ATT in a pediatric patient.</p><p>Case Presentation:</p><p>An 11-year-old boy presented with perinephric abscess with intracavitary calculus, requiring intravenous antibiotics and percutaneous drainage. The patient developed persistently draining sinus following removal of the drainage tube. Further investigations revealed a diagnosis of genitourinary tuberculosis. He completed standard ATT and his sinus drainage stopped. However, soon after completing ATT, he developed persistent drainage from the nephrocutaneous sinus. Surgical exploration and removal of the calcific foci led to complete healing of the sinus while preserving the kidney.</p><p>Conclusion:</p><p>Calculus within a tubercular abscess and subsequent sinus tract can act as a persistent inflammatory nidus and prevent healing. Careful imaging and surgical exploration is essential to cure these cases of non-healing sinus.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-64\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 64</p><h3>Overcoming the median lobe challenge in minimally invasive of Benign Prostatic Enlargement: A hybrid approach with limited median lobe resection.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Puneet Sharma, Dr. Ritesh Goel, Dr. Karandeep Guleria, Dr.Mrigank S Jha, Dr. M Umar Khan Sherwani</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super Speciality Hospital Saket</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo evaluate the functional outcomes of a hybrid approach combining minimally invasive therapy with limited median-lobe resection using Holmium laser for men with lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) with benign prostatic enlargement (BPE) and a median lobe while preserving antegrade ejaculation.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\n4 men underwent a hybrid procedure with limited median-lobe resection using Holmium laser. 3 patients underwent prostatic urethral lift procedure and 1 patient underwent Rezūm. Mean-age, prostate volume and median-lobe size were noted. IPSS, IPSS-QoL, maximum flow rate (Q-max) and post-void residual (PVR) were assessed preoperatively and at 1-month follow-up. Early postoperative complications and ejaculatory function were assessed. Pre- and postoperative functional parameters were compared using a paired Student's t-test.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nThe mean age was 54.0 ± 3.2 years, mean prostate volume 45.2 ± 9.7 mL, and mean median-lobe size 14.2 ± 7.8 mm. Mean IPSS decreased from 23.25 ± 4.57 to 5.00 ± 0.82 (p=0.0063), while IPSS-QoL improved from 4.75 ± 0.96 to 1.00 ± 0.00 (p=0.0043). Mean Qmax increased from 5.20 ± 2.81 ml/sec to 27.00 ± 5.72 mL/s (p=0.0047), and PVR<br/>\ndecreased from 382.5 ± 155.9ml to 14.5 ± 4.4 mL (p=0.0189). Antegrade ejaculation was preserved in all four patients (100%). No postoperative urinary retention, UTI or readmission was recorded.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nEarly observation suggests that combining minimally invasive treatment of BPE with limited median-lobe resection using Holmium laser can provide substantial improvement in LUTS, quality of life, urinary flow and bladder emptying while preserving ejaculation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-65\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 65</p><h3>When the Kidney Bleeds Through the Gut: RCC presenting as GI Bleed</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Siddharth Garg, Dr Shiv Charan Navriya</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo report a rare presentation of right-sided clear cell renal cell carcinoma (RCC) directly invading the duodenum and manifesting as gastrointestinal bleeding (melena), and to highlight the diagnostic challenges and multidisciplinary management required for this locally advanced disease.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nThis study evaluates a single clinical case of a patient presenting with melena in the absence of classical urological symptoms such as haematuria or flank pain. The diagnostic pathway included upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CECT). Following multidisciplinary review, the intervention performed was an aggressive en bloc radical nephrectomy combined with a pancreaticoduodenectomy. Outcome measures included surgical margin status and survival benefit.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nUpper gastrointestinal endoscopy identified bleeding originating from the second part of the duodenum without a primary mucosal lesion, indicating extrinsic invasion. Subsequent CECT demonstrated a large right renal mass directly infiltrating the adjacent duodenum, while the pancreas remained uninvolved and there was no evidence of distant metastasis. Histopathological examination confirmed clear cell RCC with direct duodenal invasion. The malignancy was definitively staged as pT4N0, and all surgical margins were negative.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nDuodenal involvement in RCC reflects advanced disease, and presentation with melena can easily mimic primary gastrointestinal pathology, creating a diagnostic dilemma. Timely radiological imaging and early immunohistochemical confirmation are crucial. In carefully selected patients without systemic disease, a coordinated multidisciplinary approach—combining aggressive en-bloc multi-visceral surgical extirpation with prompt evaluation for modern adjuvant immunotherapy—is the cornerstone of management. This strategy eradicates micro-metastatic disease, achieves sound oncological outcomes, and offers a durable survival benefit in highly aggressive pT4 disease.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-66\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 66</p><h3>PENILE FRACTURE WITH CONCOMITANT URETHRAL INJURY: SURGICAL DECISION-MAKING, INTRAOPERATIVE URETHRAL ASSESSMENT, AND FUNCTIONAL OUTCOMES</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Abhay Garg , Dr Madhur Anand , Dr Sanjay P Dhangar , Dr Ajay Lakhe , Dr Piyush Sandesh Shah , Dr Aniket Vaman Chaware</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Bharati Vidyapeeth University Medical College Hospital and Research Centre</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>BACKGROUND<br/>\n Penile fracture is a rare but true urological emergency resulting from rupture of the tunica albuginea during erection.<br/>\n Delay in diagnosis or conservative management is associated with erectile dysfunction,penile curvature and psychological distress.<br/>\n Early surgical repair remains the gold standard<br/>\nAIM<br/>\n To highlight clinical presentation and diagnostic clues of penile fracture<br/>\n To emphasize the importance of early surgical exploration<br/>\n To discuss surgical technique and postoperative outcomes<br/>\nCASE PRESENTATION<br/>\n A man In his 40s presented to EMD with complaints of pain and swelling over penile shaft associated with h/o penile trauma during sexual intercourse leading to typical cracking sound, rapid detumescence and discolouration of penis.<br/>\n On examination- typical eggplant deformity present with deviation of penis towards right and blood at meatus. No bruise or ecchymosis over scrotum,perineum and suprapubic regions.<br/>\n Usg Penis- Hematoma formation on ventral and left lateral border ,2.3mm and 2.5mm defect in tunica albuginea and buck’s fascia of left corpora cavernosa along left lateral border and along ventral surface just proximal to glans respectively and Tear in medial wall of both corpora cavernosa in mid penile shaft region<br/>\n Surgical management done -Tear present in left corpora cavernosa approx. 2cm on medial side(repaired in interrupted fashion with prolene 3-0) and Urethral tear present on ventral aspect (repaired in interrupted fashion with vicryl 3-0 over silicon foley catheter)<br/>\nCONCLUSION<br/>\n Penile fracture is a true urological emergency that should be diagnosed clinically and managed with early surgical exploration.<br/>\n Prompt tunical repair results in excellent anatomical, functional, and psychosexual outcomes while minimizing long-term complications.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-67\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 67</p><h3>Accuracy of ChatGPT in Answering Parental Questions About Posterior Urethral Valves: An Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Alok Dutta,Dr Anupam Shukla,Dr Prakhar Pratap,Dr.M.S.Ansari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS,LUCKNOW</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Importance<br/>\nParents of children with posterior urethral valves (PUV) frequently seek accessible information regarding diagnosis, treatment, and long-term outcomes. Artificial intelligence (AI) tools such as ChatGPT are increasingly used for this purpose; however, the accuracy, completeness, and consistency of such information remain uncertain.<br/>\nObjective<br/>\nTo evaluate the accuracy and completeness of ChatGPT-generated responses to common parental questions about PUV.<br/>\nDesign, Setting, and Participants<br/>\nThis cross-sectional observational study assessed ChatGPT-generated responses to 14 commonly asked parental questions regarding PUV. Questions were derived from clinical interactions at a tertiary care pediatric urology center. Responses were evaluated by 25 pediatric urology experts with at least 5 years of post-qualification experience. Experts independently rated each response using a four-category scale (misleading, helpful but incomplete, accurate, or accurate and comprehensive) and were blinded to each other’s assessments.<br/>\nMain Outcomes and Measures<br/>\nThe primary outcome was the distribution of expert ratings across response categories. Secondary analyses explored patterns of agreement and the frequency of incomplete or potentially misleading responses.</p><p>Results<br/>\nAll questions were considered relevant. Among 350 expert evaluations, 67.7% of responses were rated as accurate and comprehensive, 18.9% as helpful but incomplete, and 13.4% as potentially misleading. Eight responses received unanimous high-quality ratings, whereas six demonstrated variability in expert assessment, primarily due to omission of clinically important details such as key investigations and aspects of long-term management.<br/>\nInter-rater reliability demonstrated substantial agreement (Fleiss’ κ = 0.70).</p><p>Conclusions and Relevance<br/>\nChatGPT-generated responses demonstrated variable accuracy, with a substantial proportion lacking important clinical details. While the model may provide general explanations to common parental queries, its outputs can be incomplete and may vary across instances, limiting reliability for independent medical guidance. These findings support a limited, supplementary role for AI-generated information and reinforce the continued necessity of clinician oversight in patient education.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-68\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 68</p><h3>From Initial Extra-Peritoneal Leak to Functional Restoration: Successful Transpubic Reconstructive Management of Post-Traumatic Bladder Neck Obliteration</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Gajendra pratap singh, Prof Dr. Hemant Kumar Goel</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">ABVIMS AND DR RML HOSPITAL DELHI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>From Initial Extra-Peritoneal Leak to Functional Restoration: Successful Transpubic Reconstructive Management of Post-Traumatic Bladder Neck Obliteration</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-69\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 69</p><h3>Post-chemotherapy retroperitoneal Mass with IVC invasion</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Pravin Gehlot, Dr. Hemant Kumar Goel</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">RML HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Post-chemotherapy retroperitoneal Mass with IVC invasion</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-70\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 70</p><h3>Post-chemotherapy retroperitoneal Mass with IVC invasion</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Pravin Gehlot, Dr. Hemant Goel</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">RML HOSPITAL DELHI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Post-chemotherapy retroperitoneal Mass with IVC invasion</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-71\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 71</p><h3>Simultaneous Plaque Excision, Bovine Pericardial Grafting and Malleable Penile Prosthesis in Severe Peyronie’s Disease: A Case Report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Venkatesh Kumar, Dr Sadiq Husain, Dr Pragnesh Desai, Dr Devi Chauhan, Dr Trushar Baman</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Yatharth Superspeciality Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo describe the surgical management and clinical outcome of a patient with severe Peyronie’s disease (PD) presenting with disabling penile curvature despite preserved erectile function, using plaque excision, bovine pericardial grafting, and simultaneous malleable penile prosthesis implantation.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 45-year-old man with an 18-month history of progressive penile deformity was evaluated. Clinical assessment revealed a stable dorsal penile curvature approaching 90° and a palpable dorsal plaque measuring approximately 2 × 4 cm. Although erections were adequate for rigidity, the severe deformity prevented penetrative intercourse. Following detailed counseling regarding available surgical options, the patient underwent plaque excision with reconstruction of the resultant tunical defect using a bovine pericardial graft, combined with simultaneous implantation of a malleable penile prosthesis. Artificial erection testing was performed intraoperatively to assess correction of curvature.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nFollowing plaque excision and graft reconstruction, artificial erection testing demonstrated complete correction of the penile curvature. The postoperative period was uneventful, with no major complications reported. At follow-up, the patient had a straight penis with restoration of penetrative sexual function and reported high satisfaction. The combined procedure also provided immediate penile straightening while addressing the functional limitations associated with the severe deformity.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nSevere Peyronie’s disease with marked penile curvature can cause significant functional impairment even in patients with preserved erectile function. In carefully selected patients, plaque excision with bovine pericardial graft reconstruction combined with malleable penile prosthesis implantation may provide effective correction of severe deformity and restoration of penetrative sexual function. This approach may also help minimize the potential risk of postoperative erectile deterioration following extensive plaque excision.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-72\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 72</p><h3>INNOVATIVE MULTI-DOT TECHNIQUE FOR PEYRONIE'S DISEASE</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Hardev Singh Bhatyal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">blk max superspecility hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nTo describe a modified multi-dot plication technique for severe combined dorsal and left lateral penile curvature in stable Peyronie’s disease (PD), emphasizing the need to tailor the number and distribution of plication sutures rather than routinely applying the standard 16-dot technique.</p><p>Method:<br/>\nA 38-year-old man with stable PD presented with approximately 90° dorsal and left lateral penile curvature, making vaginal penetration impossible. Tadalafil-induced erection confirmed the severity and direction of the deformity. A firm dorsal midline plaque measuring approximately 2.0 × 1.5 cm was confirmed by penile ultrasonography. Through a ventral midline approach, the tunica albuginea of both corpora cavernosa was exposed without complete penile degloving. For correction of the dorsal curvature, eight plication points were placed at the 6 o’clock position on the right corpus cavernosum and four corresponding points on the left, with approximately 1 cm spacing. An additional four-dot plication was placed at the 8 o’clock position on the right corpus cavernosum. Thus tailoring the established technique rather than following a conventional symmetrical 16-dot configuration. Correction was assessed using intraoperative artificial erection.</p><p>Result:<br/>\nNear-complete correction of both dorsal and left lateral curvature was achieved. The strategic redistribution of plication sutures allowed targeted correction of the two distinct components of the deformity while avoiding unnecessary placement of sutures in unaffected areas. The ventral approach provided adequate exposure without complete penile degloving. Erectile function was preserved, and the patient subsequently resumed satisfactory sexual activity.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nThis case highlights that the standard 16-dot plication concept may require modification in patients with severe multidirectional penile curvature. Individualizing the number and more importantly, the location and distribution of plication sutures according to the vector of curvature may provide more effective correction while limiting unnecessary tunical manipulation. The modified multi-dot approach appears promising for selected patients with complex Peyronie’s deformity.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-73\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 73</p><h3>Single-stage management of a right iliac fossa undescended testis with prosthetic testicular implantation and contralateral microsurgical varicocelectomy in a young adult</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr S middha, Dr D Bansal , Dr s mittal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sdmh jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo describe the feasibility of single-stage management of a longstanding intra-abdominal/iliac fossa undescended testis with laparoscopic orchiectomy and immediate testicular prosthesis implantation, combined with contralateral microsurgical varicocelectomy in a young adult.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nA 20-year-old male presented with a non-palpable right undescended testis and left-sided varicocele. Imaging localized the right testis to the right iliac fossa. Considering the patient's age and increased malignancy risk associated with longstanding cryptorchidism, laparoscopic right orchiectomy was performed. In the same sitting, a right silicone testicular prosthesis was placed through a scrotal approach in the dependent hemiscrotum. Left microsurgical subinguinal varicocelectomy was subsequently performed with preservation of the testicular artery, lymphatics and vas deferens.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe combined procedure was completed successfully with minimal blood loss and no perioperative complications. The prosthesis was appropriately positioned, restoring scrotal symmetry. The patient had an uneventful postoperative recovery and was discharged in stable condition. Histopathological examination of the excised undescended testis was performed; the result should be added once available.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nSingle-stage management combining laparoscopic orchiectomy, immediate prosthetic testicular implantation and contralateral microsurgical varicocelectomy is feasible in carefully selected young adults. This approach addresses the oncological risk of longstanding cryptorchidism while simultaneously providing a cosmetic replacement and treating a potentially compromising contralateral varicocele.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-74\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 74</p><h3>A Metachronous Renal Squamous Cell Carcinoma Secondary to Esophagus squamous cell carcinoma: A Rare Case of Renal Squamous Cell Carcinoma Mimicking Renal Cell Carcinoma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Nitin Kumar Jhalani</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">JLN Medical college &amp; attached hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction</p><p>Primary renal squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) accounts for &lt;1% of renal malignancies and is usually associated with chronic irritation, nephrolithiasis, or infection. Renal metastasis from esophageal SCC is rare. We present an unusual metachronous renal SCC following treated esophageal SCC.</p><p>Method</p><p>A patient with progressive dysphagia and weight loss was diagnosed with well-to-moderately differentiated esophageal SCC. FDG-PET/CT showed an esophageal lesion with lymphadenopathy and a normal kidney. The patient underwent neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy followed by esophagectomy in July 2024. Histopathology showed no residual disease (ypT0N0). In January 2026, the patient presented with gross hematuria, right flank swelling, and pain. MRI revealed a 60 × 68 × 74 mm right renal lesion infiltrating the pelvicalyceal system. Radical nephrectomy was planned.</p><p>Results</p><p>Nephrectomy in January 2026 revealed moderately differentiated SCC extending to the capsule and perirenal fat. Chemotherapy was advised but declined due to poor general condition. The patient died in May 2026.</p><p>Conclusion</p><p>Renal SCC can mimic renal cell carcinoma. In patients with previous SCC, metastatic disease should be considered. Histopathology is essential for diagnosis, with multidisciplinary evaluation and long-term surveillance recommended.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-75\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 75</p><h3>SARCOMATOID RENAL CELL CARCINOMA : A RARE CASE REPORT</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Vishnu Kumar Gahlot</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">JLN Medical college &amp; attached hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction<br/>\nSarcomatoid renal cell carcinoma (sRCC) is a rare but highly aggressive variant of RCC, characterized by spindle‑cell morphology and loss of epithelial differentiation. Sarcomatoid differentiation is consistently associated with poor prognosis irrespective of RCC subtype, and surgical resection remains the mainstay for localized disease, though outcomes are limited due to aggressive biology and early metastasis. Median survival in advanced SRCC is reported as 5–12 months.</p><p>Method<br/>\nA 71‑year‑old male presented with complaints of gross hematuria and progressive swelling in the left flank. On clinical examination, a large, firm mass was palpable in the left flank region, measuring approximately 10 × 8 cm.<br/>\nImaging studies demonstrated a large heterogenous renal mass arising from the left kidney, with areas of necrosis and possible local extension. Based on these findings, the patient was planned for radical nephrectomy.</p><p>Results<br/>\nLeft radical nephrectomy was performed. Histopathological examination of the specimen confirmed sarcomatoid renal cell carcinoma, a rare and aggressive variant of RCC.<br/>\nImmunohistochemistry demonstrated positivity for CK7 and CD117, with sarcomatoid cells highlighted by Vimentin and focal CD10 positivity. Tumor cells were negative for AMACR. Overall impression was chromophobe RCC with sarcomatoid differentiation.</p><p>Discussion<br/>\nSarcomatoid RCC is associated with poor prognosis and resistance to conventional systemic therapy. Presence of &gt;50% sarcomatoid component, as in our case, indicates aggressive biology. Literature suggests that even &lt;10% sarcomatoid differentiation worsens survival.<br/>\nSRCC demonstrates EMT features, high Ki‑67 proliferation index, and PD‑L1 overexpression. These molecular signatures explain its aggressive nature and potential responsiveness to immunotherapy.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nSRCC is a rare, aggressive variant of RCC. Early recognition, complete surgical excision, and integration of histopathology are essential. Future directions include molecular profiling, targeted therapies, and immunotherapy combinations.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-76\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 76</p><h3>Spontaneous per urethral expulsion of a giant lower ureteric calculus in a female following percutaneous nephrostomy: A rare case report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Vishnu Kumar Gahlot</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">JLN Medical college &amp; attached hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction<br/>\nGiant ureteric stones are extremely rare and usually require surgical intervention such as ureterolithotomy or endoscopic removal. Spontaneous per urethral expulsion of giant stone in a female following PCN has rarely been reported. We describe a rare and unexpected outcome in a patient with pyonephrosis and renal failure.</p><p>Method<br/>\nA 50 yrs old female patient presented to our department with fever, left flank pain, and decreased urine output of 1-week duration.<br/>\nLaboratory investigations showed sepsis and impaired renal function with serum creatinine 5.6 mg/dL. Plain X ray KUB confirmed a radiopaque lower ureteric stone measuring approximately 3 × 1.5 cm.Ultrasonography demonstrated left sided hydronephrosis with internal echoes suggestive of pyonephrosis . A large calculus (approx. size 30 mm) was identified in the left lower ureter.<br/>\nManagement was discussed with her relatives &amp; emergency left PCN was planned.</p><p>Results<br/>\nLeft PCN was performed, draining purulent urine (approx. 250 ml). The patient showed rapid clinical improvement with normalization of inflammatory parameters and recovery of renal function (serum creatinine decreased to 1.7 mg/dL). 9 days after PCN placement, the patient spontaneously expelled the ureteric stone. Follow up X-ray KUB imaging confirmed that the stone has passed and the nephrostomy tube was removed.</p><p>Discussion<br/>\nSeeing the large collection on USG and considering the low likelihood of adequate drainage of such a significant pus collection through a ureteric stent, we decided to forgo a trial of ureteric stent and proceeded directly with percutaneous nephrostomy. PCN effectively reduces intrarenal pressure, relieves obstruction, and controls infection. Reduction in ureteric wall edema and restoration of ureteric peristalsis after diversion may facilitate stone migration in rare circumstances.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nSpontaneous per urethral expulsion of giant lower ureteric stone following PCN for pyonephrosis is rare but possible. Repeat imaging and reassessment after diversion are essential to avoid unnecessary surgical intervention.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-77\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 77</p><h3>Transverse Testicular Ectopia: What Urologists Must Know — Insights in Embryology and Management</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shreyas Bindal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">GMC KOTA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo report a rare case of transverse testicular ectopia (TTE), an anomaly of testicular descent in<br/>\nwhich the ectopic testis lies in the contralateral groin or hemiscrotum, and to highlight its<br/>\nembryological basis, diagnostic approach, and surgical management.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 30-year-old male labourer presented with a painless, gradually enlarging right inguinoscrotal<br/>\nswelling of two years' duration, with an empty left hemiscrotum. Examination revealed a large<br/>\nreducible right indirect inguinal hernia, a well-developed right hemiscrotum, and a poorly<br/>\ndeveloped, empty left hemiscrotum. Ultrasonography confirmed both testes on the right side —<br/>\none in the inguinal canal, the other at the deep inguinal ring — with no Müllerian structures on<br/>\nthe left, confirmed on diagnostic laparoscopy.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nOpen inguinal exploration showed both testes within a common meso-orchium, separated by 4<br/>\ncm from the hernia sac. Bilateral orchiopexy and hernia repair were performed: the right testis<br/>\nwas fixed in the ipsilateral subdartos pouch, and the left testis was transferred through a<br/>\ntrans-septal window and fixed in the left subdartos pouch, with mesh hernioplasty. Both testes<br/>\nwere normal on Doppler with no vascular compromise, and the patient remained asymptomatic<br/>\non follow-up.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nTTE typically presents as an inguinal hernia with contralateral or bilateral cryptorchidism, and<br/>\nis often missed preoperatively due to low awareness. Clinical suspicion supported by USG<br/>\nenables preoperative diagnosis. When vas deferens and vascular length are adequate,<br/>\ntransseptal orchiopexy allows single-stage correction; surgeons managing inguinal hernias<br/>\nshould recognize this entity.<br/>\nConflict of Interest<br/>\nNo conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-78\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 78</p><h3>Transverse Testicular Ectopia: What Urologist must know- insights in Embryology and Management</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shreyas Bindal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">GMC KOTA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Transverse Testicular Ectopia: What Urologists Must Know<br/>\n— Insights in Embryology and Management<br/>\nPresented By: Dr Shreyas Bindal (MCh Resident)<br/>\nUnder guidance of:<br/>\nDr Nilesh Kumar Jain (Principal and Controller, Senior Professor Urology Department, Govt.<br/>\nMedical College Kota)<br/>\nDr Shailendra Goyal (Head of Department and Professor, Urology, Govt. Medical College<br/>\nKota)<br/>\nAim<br/>\nTo report a rare case of transverse testicular ectopia (TTE), an anomaly of testicular descent in<br/>\nwhich the ectopic testis lies in the contralateral groin or hemiscrotum, and to highlight its<br/>\nembryological basis, diagnostic approach, and surgical management.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 30-year-old male labourer presented with a painless, gradually enlarging right inguinoscrotal<br/>\nswelling of two years' duration, with an empty left hemiscrotum. Examination revealed a large<br/>\nreducible right indirect inguinal hernia, a well-developed right hemiscrotum, and a poorly<br/>\ndeveloped, empty left hemiscrotum. Ultrasonography confirmed both testes on the right side —<br/>\none in the inguinal canal, the other at the deep inguinal ring — with no Müllerian structures on<br/>\nthe left, confirmed on diagnostic laparoscopy.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nOpen inguinal exploration showed both testes within a common meso-orchium, separated by 4<br/>\ncm from the hernia sac. Bilateral orchiopexy and hernia repair were performed: the right testis<br/>\nwas fixed in the ipsilateral subdartos pouch, and the left testis was transferred through a<br/>\ntrans-septal window and fixed in the left subdartos pouch, with mesh hernioplasty. Both testes<br/>\nwere normal on Doppler with no vascular compromise, and the patient remained asymptomatic<br/>\non follow-up.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nTTE typically presents as an inguinal hernia with contralateral or bilateral cryptorchidism, and<br/>\nis often missed preoperatively due to low awareness. Clinical suspicion supported by USG<br/>\nenables preoperative diagnosis. When vas deferens and vascular length are adequate,<br/>\ntransseptal orchiopexy allows single-stage correction; surgeons managing inguinal hernias<br/>\nshould recognize this entity.<br/>\nConflict of Interest<br/>\nNo conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-79\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 79</p><h3>Lust Meet’s the Toolbox: Penile Strangulation following Non-Conventional Sexual Practice</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Parveen, Dr Nilesh kumar jain, Dr Shailendra Goyal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Govt medical college kota</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo report an unusual case of penile strangulation caused by an iron hammer head entrapped at the base of the penis, and to describe its surgical management and outcome.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nA 24-year-old male presented with an iron hammer head stuck at the base of the penis for several hours prior to presentation, with associated distal swelling of penis. On examination, the hammer head was firmly encircling the penile root, with erythema and edema of the shaft, glans, and prepuce. After doing the baseline investigations, the patient was taken up for emergency surgery under appropriate anesthesia. Bilateral lateral relieving incisions were given to reduce edema and constriction, and careful removal of hammer head. A tunica albuginea defect was identified and repaired with prolene 4-0 sutures. No urethral injury was present. Excessively swollen/excoriated skin was excised and circumcision was done. A 16 Fr per urethral foley catheter was placed.</p><p>Results<br/>\nThe foreign body (hammer head) was successfully removed with preservation of penile viability. No fresh bleeding or major vascular compromise was noted intra-operatively. The patient remained vitally stable postoperatively. He was managed with daily dressings, antibiotics, anti-inflammatory drugs, and was discharged in stable condition on postoperative day 3 after removal of per urethral catheter. Patient was followed up after 10 days, wound was healthy and no complain by the patient.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nPenile strangulation by an accidentally or intentionally entrapped rigid object is a urological emergency requiring early recognition and surgical intervention to prevent vascular compromise, tissue necrosis, or long-term sexual dysfunction. Timely relieving incisions and careful foreign body extraction, combined with structured wound care, can achieve good outcomes with organ preservation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-80\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 80</p><h3>Growing Teratoma Syndrome Following Chemotherapy for Metastatic Non-Seminomatous Germ Cell Tumour: Successful Multidisciplinary Surgical Management of a Rare Case</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Satyendra Singh, Dr Harshit Garg</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Indraprastha Apollo Haospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nGrowing Teratoma Syndrome (GTS) is a rare clinical entity characterized by progressive enlargement of metastatic masses despite normalization of serum tumour markers following systemic chemotherapy for non-seminomatous germ cell tumours (NSGCT). Early recognition is crucial, as complete surgical excision remains the only curative treatment. We report a challenging case of post-chemotherapy GTS managed successfully with multidisciplinary surgery.<br/>\nMethodology:<br/>\nA retrospective review of the patient's clinical records, imaging studies, serum tumour markers, operative findings, and histopathological reports was performed. Clinical presentation, oncological treatment, radiological progression, and surgical outcomes were analysed.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nA 23-year-old man presented with a left testicular mixed NSGCT (60% embryonal carcinoma, 35% teratoma, 5% yolk sac tumour; pT2, LVI positive) associated with bulky retroperitoneal and left supraclavicular nodal metastases. Initial tumour markers were markedly elevated (AFP 2235 ng/mL, β-hCG 2880 mIU/mL). Following left high inguinal orchiectomy and four cycles of BEP chemotherapy, serum tumour markers normalized; however, serial FDG PET-CT demonstrated progressive enlargement of both retroperitoneal and cervical masses, with compression of the inferior vena cava and left renal vein, consistent with GTS. The patient underwent classical retroperitoneal lymph node dissection with complete excision of the left supraclavicular mass and recovered uneventfully.<br/>\nDiscussion:<br/>\nThis case highlights the importance of recognizing enlarging residual masses with normalized tumour markers as GTS rather than chemotherapy-resistant disease. Complete surgical resection offers excellent long-term disease control and should be undertaken in experienced high-volume centres through a multidisciplinary approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-81\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 81</p><h3>Urinary Schistosomiasis Masquerading as Bladder Carcinoma in Northern India</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Pratik Gond, Dr Shikhar Aggarwal, Dr Rajeev Sarpal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">HIMS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Urinary schistosomiasis is a rare differential diagnosis for painless hematuria in non-endemic regions and may mimic bladder carcinoma. We report a challenging case highlighting diagnostic and therapeutic implications.<br/>\nMaterial and Methods: A 60-year-old male presented with gross painless hematuria, anemia and left hydronephrosis. Clinical examination, laboratory investigations, ultrasonography, CT urography, cystoscopy and histopathology following transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) were reviewed. Management included antiparasitic therapy followed by restage TURBT.<br/>\nResults: Imaging demonstrated multiple polypoidal bladder masses involving the left vesicoureteric junction, highly suspicious for malignancy. Histopathology unexpectedly revealed calcified parasitic eggs consistent with Schistosoma haematobium without evidence of malignancy. The patient received praziquantel with planned endoscopic reassessment. This case emphasizes that urinary schistosomiasis can present as pseudotumoral bladder lesions causing hematuria, obstruction and renal dysfunction.<br/>\nConclusion: Urinary schistosomiasis should be considered in patients with unexplained hematuria and bladder masses, particularly when histopathology is discordant with imaging. Early tissue diagnosis prevents overtreatment and timely praziquantel therapy can avert irreversible urinary tract damage. Awareness of this uncommon entity is essential for urologists.<br/>\nKeywords: Urinary schistosomiasis; Schistosoma haematobium; Hematuria; Bladder mass; TURBT; Praziquantel.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-82\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 82</p><h3>Redefining Eligibility in Renal Transplantation: Promise and Pitfalls of Marginal Donors and Recipients With Multiple Myeloma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Pratik Gond, Dr Shikhar A, Dr Rajeev S, Dr Rishin</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">HIMS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Objective<br/>\nThe growing disparity between the demand and availability of donor kidneys has led to increased utilization of marginal donors and expanded-criteria grafts in renal<br/>\ntransplantation. Simultaneously, advances in the management of multiple myeloma have<br/>\nimproved survival and opened new avenues for kidney transplantation in carefully selected<br/>\npatients previously considered unsuitable candidates. We aimed to highlight the evolving<br/>\nrole of marginal donors and complex recipients in renal transplantation through a rare and<br/>\nchallenging clinical scenario.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nWe present a case review of renal transplantation performed in a recipient with end-stage kidney disease and prior multiple myeloma, utilizing a marginal donor graft after<br/>\ncomprehensive multidisciplinary evaluation. Donor and recipient selection was performed<br/>\nafter detailed medical, nephrological, oncological, and surgical assessment. Relevant<br/>\nliterature regarding expanded-criteria donors, marginal living donors, and transplantation<br/>\noutcomes in multiple myeloma patients was reviewed to evaluate current evidence and challenges associated with such transplants.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nKidney disease is seen in nearly half of patients with multiple myeloma, with a significant<br/>\nproportion progressing to dialysis-dependent renal failure. Recent advances in<br/>\nimmunomodulatory therapy and disease control have improved long-term survival, making<br/>\ntransplantation feasible in selected low-risk patients with sustained remission. Use of<br/>\nmarginal donors increased access to transplantation and reduced waiting time. Successful<br/>\ntransplantation was achieved with satisfactory graft function and clinical recovery. Literature<br/>\nreview suggests that carefully selected recipients with controlled multiple myeloma can<br/>\nachieve favorable outcomes after transplantation when supported by meticulous<br/>\nimmunosuppressive planning and close follow-up.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nThe use of marginal donors can substantially expand the donor pool and improve access to<br/>\nrenal transplantation. selected patients with multiple<br/>\nmyeloma should no longer be considered absolute contraindications for transplantation.<br/>\nCareful recipient selection, adequate disease-free interval, multidisciplinary<br/>\nperioperative management are essential for successful outcomes. This case adds to<br/>\nlimited literature supporting renal transplantation in complex marginal scenarios</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-83\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 83</p><h3>Delayed Renal Artery Pseudoaneurysm Following Grade IV Blunt Renal Trauma in an Adolescent: Successful Endovascular Management</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Saurabh Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">HIMS Dehradun</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nRenal trauma constitutes approximately 1–5% of all traumatic injuries, with blunt abdominal trauma being the commonest etiology. High-grade renal injuries are increasingly managed conservatively in hemodynamically stable patients. However, delayed complications such as renal artery pseudoaneurysm and secondary hemorrhage remain uncommon but potentially life-threatening conditions requiring prompt recognition and intervention.<br/>\nAim:<br/>\nTo highlight the successful diagnosis and minimally invasive management of delayed renal pseudoaneurysm presenting with gross hematuria following conservatively managed Grade IV blunt renal trauma.<br/>\nMethod:<br/>\nA 16-year-old male presented with history of fall from approximately 30 feet while trekking and was diagnosed with Grade IV right renal injury with perirenal hematoma. Initially, he was hemodynamically stable and managed conservatively with intravenous fluids, antibiotics, analgesics, and serial hemoglobin monitoring. Follow-up ultrasonography demonstrated resolving renal injury and hematoma. One month later, the patient developed sudden painful gross hematuria with clot retention. Contrast enhanced CT urography revealed a small pseudoaneurysm with active contrast extravasation from the lower pole of the right kidney.<br/>\nResult:<br/>\nThe patient underwent urgent selective angioembolization by the interventional radiology team followed by cystopanendoscopy and clot evacuation under spinal anesthesia. Approximately 30–40 cc bladder clot was evacuated, with no active bleeding visualized intraoperatively. Post-procedure recovery was uneventful. Hematuria resolved completely, renal function remained preserved, and the patient was discharged in stable condition with planned follow-up imaging.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nDelayed hematuria following high-grade renal trauma should raise suspicion for renal pseudoaneurysm. Early imaging and prompt angioembolization can successfully control bleeding, preserve renal function, and avoid nephrectomy in hemodynamically stable patients.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-84\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 84</p><h3>Imaging-Suspected Adrenal Adenoma Versus Primary Adrenal Lymphoma: A Diagnostic Dilemma in an Adrenal Mass</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Saurabh Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">HIMS DEHRADUN</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Adrenal masses are commonly detected on imaging, and most are benign adenomas. CT scan is usually used to differentiate benign from malignant lesions. However, some lesions, especially lipid-poor adenomas, can appear atypical and mimic malignancy. Primary adrenal lymphoma is a rare condition and does not have specific imaging features, which makes diagnosis difficult. Correct diagnosis is important because treatment differs significantly from other adrenal tumors.<br/>\nCase Details: A 49-year-old male presented with left flank pain for 5 days. He had no urinary symptoms or significant medical history. Routine blood tests were normal. Ultrasound and contrast-enhanced CT showed a heterogeneous mass in the left adrenal region with hemorrhagic and enhancing components, suggesting a lipid-poor adrenal adenoma. Hormonal evaluation was normal. Due to ongoing symptoms, surgery was planned. The patient later returned with severe pain, and repeat imaging showed that the mass had increased in size with more hemorrhage. During surgery, a large, vascular mass with dense adhesions was found, and left adrenalectomy with nephrectomy was performed. Histopathological examination revealed a malignant round cell tumor. Immunohistochemistry showed CD45 and CD20 positivity with a high Ki-67 index (60–70%), confirming diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.<br/>\nConclusion: Imaging alone may not always give the correct diagnosis in adrenal masses. Rare conditions like primary adrenal lymphoma can mimic benign lesions. Histopathology is essential for definitive diagnosis, and a multidisciplinary approach helps in proper management.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"poster-85\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Poster Abstract 85</p><h3>Understanding pharmacotherapy for treating mixed (predominant storage) LUTS/BPH: A pan-India survey among urologists (MiLUTS Survey)</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Sameer Trivedi,1 Prachi Kundaikar,2 Akshay Nabar,2 Madhumita Panda2</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS, BHU, Varanasi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>OBJECTIVE: Assessing Indian urologists’ perspectives on burden, patient profiles, apt diagnostic tools and pharmacotherapy in mixed LUTS/BPH.<br/>\nMATERIALS &amp; METHODS: E-survey among 356 urologists pan-India.<br/>\nRESULTS:<br/>\nAround 58% urologists stated 21-40% of their LUTS/BPH patients to have mixed LUTS/BPH, with 85% stating these patients to be aged between 50-70 years. 60.4% and 48.31% stated nocturia (≥3/night by 83.7% urologists) and urgency (2-4/day by 66.57%) respectively to be the top 2 bothersome storage symptoms. 87% considered V/S ratio (ratio of IPSS voiding-to-storage sub-score) to be a useful parameter to identify mixed LUTS/BPH, with ~83% expressing preference to have it validated.<br/>\nSilodosin-mirabegron combination (S+M) was urologists’ most preferred option (~65%) to treat mixed LUTS/BPH. 51.12%, 46.06% and 44.1% preferred S+M as upfront therapy for mixed LUTS/BPH with predominant frequency, urgency and urge incontinence respectively. 45.78% reserved it as a second-line in mixed LUTS/BPH refractory to silodosin monotherapy, which was mostly (by ~81%) tried for 2-4 weeks. Rapidity of action (by 81.74%), good overall efficacy (by 70.78%), especially for nocturia (by 55%) and safety in comorbidities including cardiovascular (by 61%) of silodosin and lack of side-effects (by 77.5%) including urinary retention (by 67.69%) with mirabegron were the primary drivers of choice of S+M. ~94% preferred 8 mg while ~68% preferred 50 mg as stable/maximal strengths of silodosin and mirabegron respectively. S+M was most commonly prescribed for 3-6 months (by 66% urologists) with 84% preferring it as FDC. In case of sub-optimal response to S+M, 44.4% urologists switched to different combinations while 36% carried out urodynamic assessment as next steps.</p><p>CONCLUSION:<br/>\nSignificant proportion of LUTS/BPH patients have mixed LUTS/BPH, with nocturia and urgency being the top 2 bothersome storage symptoms with S+M being most preferred combination as both upfront and add-on. V/S ratio can be a useful tool to identify mixed LUTS/BPH.</p></div></details></article>"

